WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.410
- I want to welcome you all
to the 2023 Castle Lectures,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.640
which are the signature
lectures for the EP&amp;E program.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.670
We had a long pause because of COVID,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.540
but we're very pleased to
finally be able to be back here

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.240
organizing these events in person.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.620
My name is Ana de la O,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.740
and I am the Director of the EP&amp;E program.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.370
And before introducing our
speaker today, Ben Wizner,

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:33.840
I want to tell you just a little bit

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.150
about these Castle Lectures.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.500
In 1988, John Castle made
a generous gift to Yale

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.450
to establish the lecture series.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.070
He did so in honor of his
ancestor, Reverend James Pierpont.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.710
He was a Harvard trained
Congregationalist minister

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:54.360
who founded the Collegiate
School of Connecticut in 1701.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.770
Soon after, that School
of Connecticut was renamed

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.250
after its benefactor, Elihu Yale.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.770
We are grateful to Mr.
Castle and his family

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.400
to the contribution to
the university, broadly,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.803
and to the EP&amp;E program, in particular.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.850
The Castle Lectures aim
to promote awareness

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.080
of the ethical challenges and
complexities of our society.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.960
And they're also meant
to foster robust dialogue

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:25.470
about the moral foundations
of society and government.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:28.350
Our speaker today is Ben Wizner.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:32.010
He's the director of the
American Civil Liberties Union's

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.220
Speech, Privacy, and Technology Project.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.980
In more than two decades at the ACLU,

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.130
Ben has litigated many significant cases

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.060
at the intersection of human
rights and national security,

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.300
including challenges to
the surveillance practices,

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.480
government security, sorry,
government watch lists,

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.490
targeted killings, and torture.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.120
Since 2013, he has been
the principal legal advisor

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.630
to NSA whistleblower Edward Snowden.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.820
Ben is a graduate of Harvard College

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.760
and New York University School of Law.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.507
His lecture series are entitled

00:02:07.507 --> 00:02:09.690
"Above the Law, or Beneath It,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.780
How National Security Law
Subverts Accountability

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.760
and Weakens Democracy."

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.650
These lecture series are going
to be divided in two parts.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.930
So this is a two day event.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.607
Today's lecture, has a title

00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:27.630
"Anatomy of a National Security Lawsuit."

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:31.260
Tomorrow we're gonna meet again
at the same time, 4:00 PM,

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.210
to hear the second part of the lecture,

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.840
which is entitled "The Case
Against 'National Security'."

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.480
Just a few words about the
format of these lectures.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.430
We're gonna let Ben present
for about 45, 50 minutes

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.180
and then we're going to have
plenty of time for questions.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.960
So I'm going to ask you to
please hold your questions

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.760
towards the end, even though I know

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.130
this is going to be a terrific

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.710
and thought-provoking (chuckles) lecture.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.080
So please, welcome me in joining Ben.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.580
And Ben, the floor is yours.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.863
- [Ben] Thank you. Thanks.
(audience applauding)

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.660
Thank you for that introduction, Ana,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.900
and thank you all for
being here this afternoon.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.720
I'm honored to be invited
to offer these lectures

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.910
in what is my hometown.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.680
One of the privileges of growing up

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.620
in a community like this
one is that you have access

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.323
to a world of expertise
outside your own family.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:29.190
When I was a 17 year
old, high school senior,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.470
nervous, a little
headstrong about heading off

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.210
to Harvard in the fall,

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.090
I asked the political
theorist, Ian Shapiro,

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.770
whether I should take the year long

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.530
Introduction to Economics, Ec 10,

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.730
which at that time was taught

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:45.153
by the renowned conservative
economist Martin Feldstein.

00:03:46.057 --> 00:03:47.340
"You could," he responded,

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.141
and I will not attempt
a South African accent,

00:03:50.141 --> 00:03:51.127
(audience chuckling)

00:03:51.127 --> 00:03:53.682
"or I could teach it to
you in one sentence."

00:03:53.682 --> 00:03:55.110
(audience laughing)

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.277
This was thrilling to hear.

00:03:57.277 --> 00:03:59.880
"What's the sentence?" I asked.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.687
His response, "Imagine the world is flat."

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:08.220
At the time, this sounded
like a cryptic koan.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.490
Only later did I appreciate
it as a glib dismissal

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.980
of the premise that human
behavior is best understood

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.253
as a series of rational
cost-benefit analyses.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.110
It's genuinely hard for me
to fathom that I am older now

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.710
than Ian Shapiro was at
the time of that exchange.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.200
And that Harvard's canonical
Ec 10 is now taught

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.518
by one of my college drinking buddies.

00:04:30.518 --> 00:04:31.470
(audience laughing)

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.310
No less boggling is that it
now falls to me and my cohort

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:38.490
to play Zen master and to
perplex and enlighten the youth.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:39.423
So here it goes.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.290
If a bright and idealistic
college graduate

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.870
on her way to law school
were to ask me today

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.990
whether she should take a
course in national security law,

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.420
I would be tempted to channel Ian Shapiro

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.390
and offer to teach it to
her in a single sentence.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:58.530
Something like, "The time
bomb is always ticking."

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.430
Or maybe, "We neither confirm nor deny

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.230
what you see in plain sight."

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:07.380
Or better still, "Heads, we win.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.667
Tails, you lose."

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.060
National security law has
not achieved the status

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.430
in law schools that
macroeconomics occupies

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.060
in our colleges and universities,
but it's on the march.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.010
In the two decades since the 9/11 attacks,

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.950
it's moved from the fringes
of law school curricula

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.950
to a privileged front and center position.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.020
New courses have proliferated.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.790
New journals have launched.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.520
Leading law schools have
announced prestigious fellowships,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.490
think tank collaborations,
and all manner of grace

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:37.023
that the academy can bestow.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.660
As a lawyer who spent the better part

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.580
of the last two decades litigating,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.980
or rather attempting to litigate,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.990
national security law cases,

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.400
I've observed these developments

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.653
with a mix of bewilderment and annoyance.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.430
To be sure, many of the
questions and controversies

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.400
that fall within the rubric
of national security law

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.680
are vital ones that deserve significant

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.840
attention and careful scrutiny.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.600
But when confronted with those
questions in recent years,

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.780
courts in the United States
have almost uniformly refused

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.160
to grapple with them.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.323
Instead, deeming them non-justiciable.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:14.340
Did military officials
violate the constitution

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.650
when they seized a U.S.
citizen on U.S. soil,

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:20.580
held him for years, incommunicado
in solitary confinement,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.470
and subjected him to beatings,

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.740
sensory deprivation, and forced drugging?

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.210
We don't know,

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.950
because federal courts in South Carolina

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.020
and Virginia decline
to answer on the ground

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:34.230
that Congress had not created
a specific cause of action

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:35.730
for alleged enemy combatants

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.430
claiming abuse in military custody.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:39.810
Are there any legal constraints

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.210
on the government's authority
to carry out targeted killings

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.400
of U.S. citizen terrorism suspects

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.070
far from any recognizable battlefield?

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:48.840
Put otherwise, can the
government carry out

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.630
the extrajudicial killing
of one of its own citizens

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:52.620
without even telling him

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:56.280
what conduct might
subject him to execution?

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.740
We don't know, because the federal court

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.660
in the District of Columbia
dismissed a lawsuit

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:03.060
on the ground that it falls
to the political branches

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:04.860
rather than the judiciary to decide

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.210
whether and when U.S. citizens
may be killed overseas.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.180
Does a federal law that
authorizes the mass acquisition

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.130
of U.S. Citizens' international telephone

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.690
and email communications

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.180
without individualized judicial warrants

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.820
violate the Constitution's
Fourth Amendment?

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.670
We don't know, because the
U.S. Supreme Court said

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.620
that the plaintiffs who brought
the challenge could not show

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.110
that their own communications
had been swept up,

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.440
and they, therefore, lack
standing to bring a lawsuit.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.990
Can a foreign citizen who
was kidnapped by the CIA

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:37.320
in a case of mistaken identity,
and imprisoned and abused

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:41.400
in a foreign dungeon claim
any remedy under U.S. law?

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.380
We don't know, because courts

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.290
in Virginia accepted the CIA's
argument that any litigation

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:48.690
of the claims would reveal state secrets

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.280
and damage national security.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:51.990
And just last month,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.960
can the National Security
Agency intercept and copy all

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.320
of Americans' international
communications including emails,

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.050
web browsing content,
search engine queries

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:02.610
by installing surveillance devices

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.830
on the Internet's backbone and then search

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:08.310
that massive database
without any court approval?

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:10.470
We don't know, because in February,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.500
the Supreme Court refused to
review a lower court ruling,

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.830
dismissing that case on secrecy grounds.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:19.530
Warrantless surveillance
of American citizens,

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.140
kidnapping and torture at home and abroad,

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:24.330
extrajudicial kill lists.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:28.353
Non-justiciable,
non-justiciable, non-justiciable.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:32.460
These outcomes deprive
litigants of remedies,

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:33.690
but of greater significance,

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:35.220
by failing to say what the law is,

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:38.910
they deprive the nation of
a definitive adjudication,

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.490
allow abuses to continue unchecked,

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:45.090
and leave the door open
for future misconduct.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.580
Even in those rare instances

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.730
in which courts have rejected
these most extreme outcomes,

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.860
most notably, when the
Supreme Court repudiated

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.020
the Bush administration's crude attempt

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.333
to overrule the Magna Carta in Guantanamo,

00:08:58.290 --> 00:08:59.730
the court's rulings have often been

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.130
more symbol than substance,

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:05.790
a court claiming authority
rather than exercising it.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:06.810
The government may have lost

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.730
three landmark Guantanamo cases,

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.340
but more than 20 years
into the Guantanamo era,

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.440
the Supreme Court still has not told us

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.653
whether indefinite detention
without trial there is lawful.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:22.290
All of this litigation, without
any substantive outcomes,

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.380
generates a kind of fog
of law that conceals

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.630
what I believe to be the
real organizing principle

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:32.100
of national security law,
impunity for senior officials.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.940
This regime is robust and
malleable and has proven adept

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.790
across decades at identifying new hosts.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.880
It will not be dislodged anytime soon.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.880
The more modest goal of this
polemic is to contribute

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.570
to a process of diminishing
its respectability.

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.030
It makes perfect sense that
the high priests of this order,

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.840
those senior officials who
float between top positions

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.790
in the intelligence community
and defense contracting,

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.320
would seek to imbue their project

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.430
with the gravity and solemnity
of an existential struggle

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:05.190
and would view challenges
to their noble efforts

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.080
as attempts to tie their hands

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.633
during what they insist
on defining as wartime.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.350
There's no good reason
why the rest of society,

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.980
least of all the academy, should
consent to that definition

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:18.873
or participate in that mythmaking.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.360
The intelligence community
uses the term "indoctrination"

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.300
to describe the process of being
sworn into the secret world

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:28.980
of security clearance.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.970
National security law, likewise,
seeks to indoctrinate us

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.040
into a profoundly anti-democratic way

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.490
of thinking about governance.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.390
National security law is organized

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.280
around the systematic
exclusion of the public

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.400
from vital decisions
about government policy.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.290
It insists that the separate
branches of government

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:49.830
that were designed to provide
a check on executive abuses

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.950
must instead defer to
the purported expertise

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.130
of the executive wrongdoers themselves.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.440
Indeed, national security
law is predicated

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:00.360
on democratic fragility.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.850
Insofar as, its central claim

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:04.560
that we need an exceptional set of rules

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.140
to address putatively exceptional threats,

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:10.117
invariably implies, in
Wendell Berry's words that,

00:11:10.117 --> 00:11:12.600
"A democracy such as ours can work only

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.637
in the most favorable circumstances."

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:16.710
It's more than a little ironic

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:18.960
that a rogues' gallery of
national security elites

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:21.630
has pulled off a nifty recent rebrand

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.340
by embracing the banner of democracy

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:24.960
after devoting their careers

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.450
to weakening its checks and balances.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.450
In tomorrow's lecture,
I'll dig a little deeper

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:32.310
into why I believe national security law

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.320
is, at best, incoherent,

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:38.370
and at worse, a carefully
cultivated deception.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:39.870
Today, I want to take you on a journey

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:41.220
deep down the rabbit hole

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.000
of one relatively obscure
national security case.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.350
But before I do that,

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:50.077
I hope you'll indulge a brief
Admiral Stockdale moment,

00:11:50.077 --> 00:11:52.740
"Who am I? What am I doing here?"

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.807
This is is my test to see who
in the room is 50 or older.

00:11:55.807 --> 00:11:58.230
Very few. (chuckling)
(audience laughing)

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:01.920
As is probably clear by now,
I don't quite fit the profile

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.960
of past lecturers in this series.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.880
I've not spent my career
reading and writing

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.070
about the issues that
I'm here to talk about.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:09.780
But rather, being battered around by them

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:11.220
in a hostile arena.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:12.453
This was not by design.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.380
I didn't set out to be a
national security lawyer.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.200
Mine was the last generation
to graduate law school

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.450
before the 9/11 attacks, and thus,

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:24.030
before our national security
law became enshrined

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:25.323
as a prominent fixture.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.010
Our graduation week class gala,

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:31.200
our so-called Last Chance Dance,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:32.940
took place at Windows on the World,

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.980
atop of the World Trade Center

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:38.339
on an exceptionally foggy
night in May of 2000.

00:12:38.339 --> 00:12:41.040
Among the many things we
couldn't see that night

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.990
was how our lives and
careers would be shaped

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:44.400
by the unthinkable events

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.530
that would unfold there a year later.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:49.620
I've often thought about
the cooks, waiters,

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.590
and cleaners who served
us and whether any of them

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:55.560
might have died in that
room one year later.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:58.050
In August of 2001, a month before the day

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.420
that ended the end of history,

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:02.883
I joined the ACLU as a staff attorney.

00:13:04.853 --> 00:13:07.297
Lionel Trilling said of
the Spanish Civil War,

00:13:07.297 --> 00:13:08.490
"Everyone knew that it was one

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.500
of the decisive events of our epic.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.050
Everyone said so at the
time it was being fought,

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:14.250
and everyone was right."

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:17.700
This fairly well captures
the sense of dread

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.280
and anticipation that we all experienced

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:22.590
in September of 2001.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:25.050
Even before wars of choice were launched,

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:26.790
prisoners were brutalized,

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.850
and our national discourse
devolved into the conceptual

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:32.553
and moral absurdity of a war on terror.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.930
The two decades of my ACLU career

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:39.840
have tracked the two
decades of this forever war

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:43.770
from Washington, D.C. courtrooms
to Guantanamo Bay, Cuba,

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.840
to the ancient capital of Yemen,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.913
and most unexpectedly, to Moscow.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:51.330
But much of that time has
been spent at my desk,

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:52.830
reading and writing legal briefs,

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:56.310
trying without success to
persuade courts to rule

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.010
on the legality of
executive actions undertaken

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:00.660
in the name of national security.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.580
There are national security lawsuits

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:07.860
that produce lofty judicial pronouncements

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.020
and fill law school casebooks.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.760
There are others that make headlines,

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:13.800
even if they don't make law.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:16.600
The case I'm going to talk
about today is none of those.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:20.640
It certainly is not among
the more important cases

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:22.230
that I lost.

00:14:22.230 --> 00:14:24.870
I've chosen it because I think
it epitomizes the pathologies

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:27.810
of national security
litigation and offers a flavor

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.750
of the style of argument
that the government employs

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.783
and courts routinely
accept in these cases.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:36.750
The lawsuit concerned a request

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:38.610
under the Freedom of Information Act

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:40.980
for a few dozen pages of documents.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:43.770
In particular, a request to
the Department of Defense

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:45.660
and the Central Intelligence Agency

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.090
for unredacted transcripts of a handful

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:50.640
of Combatant Status Review
Tribunal proceedings

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.230
at Guantanamo Bay.

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.300
If you don't know what
that sentence meant,

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:55.800
I congratulate you.
(audience laughing)

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.110
If you do, hello, old friend.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:59.830
We'll catch up after the lecture.

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:00.690
(audience laughing)

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:04.230
Before I get to the case,
I should set the stage,

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:07.130
and I apologize to those of
you for whom this is remedial.

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:10.980
The particular quasi-legal
proceedings at the center

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.530
of this dispute represent the convergence

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:16.110
of two of the more disgraceful
policy decisions made

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.480
by the Bush administration after 9/11,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.780
the attempt to create a legal
no man's land in Guantanamo

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.310
and the creation of an archipelago

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:26.043
of secret CIA run torture
prisons around the world.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:29.580
The Bush administration
opened the prison camp

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:32.520
in Guantanamo Bay in January, 2002.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:35.190
The plan of course was
to locate the prison

00:15:35.190 --> 00:15:37.650
outside the jurisdiction
of our federal courts

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:40.530
to create an island
outside the law so that men

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:44.250
and boys could be imprisoned
without legal recourse

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.920
and interrogated without legal restraint.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.530
The prisoners were prohibited
from contacting lawyers,

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.890
and lawyers were prohibited
from visiting the prison.

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.780
When human rights lawyers
found a workaround

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.850
by locating family
members of the prisoners

00:15:56.850 --> 00:16:00.300
and initiating habeas corpus
proceedings in federal courts,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.280
the government insisted
that the suits be dismissed

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:05.160
on jurisdictional grounds
with no adjudication

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.440
of whether the prisoners
had been lawfully captured

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:08.313
and detained.

00:16:09.487 --> 00:16:11.961
"Would the government's
arguments be the same,"

00:16:11.961 --> 00:16:13.777
one federal judge asked
in a hearing in 2003,

00:16:13.777 --> 00:16:15.090
"if the claims were that the government

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:17.490
was engaging in acts of torture

00:16:17.490 --> 00:16:20.257
or even extrajudicial execution?"

00:16:20.257 --> 00:16:22.117
"Yes," said the Department of Justice,

00:16:22.117 --> 00:16:23.998
"there would be no jurisdiction

00:16:23.998 --> 00:16:26.070
to consider those claims either."

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:28.200
In hindsight, it seems clear

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:29.790
that the government
would've been better off

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:33.360
inviting judicial review
rather than fighting it.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:35.670
As I mentioned earlier, two decades later

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:37.410
and after a trio of cases

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:39.060
in which the Supreme Court repudiated

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:42.030
the government's efforts to
evade any judicial review,

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.360
the court has still not answered
the fundamental question,

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:47.370
whether indefinite military detention

00:16:47.370 --> 00:16:51.270
at Guantanamo was or is lawful.

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:54.750
In other words, the court
dramatically clawed back a power

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:58.800
that it has steadfastly
declined to exercise,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.110
demonstrating a greater willingness

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:02.790
to defend its own prerogatives

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:05.343
than to interfere with executive abuses.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:08.580
In 2004, in the first
of those three cases,

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:10.380
the Supreme Court in Rasul v. Bush held

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:11.460
that because the United States

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:13.530
exercised complete sovereign control

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.330
over the Guantanamo Naval base,

00:17:15.330 --> 00:17:18.300
the prisoners there could
file habeas corpus petitions

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.100
in U.S. federal courts.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:22.560
About a month after the Rasul decision,

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:24.630
the Department of Defense established

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:26.190
the Combatant Status Review Tribunals,

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:30.120
or CSRTs, as they are
more commonly called.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.340
These were ad hoc administrative bodies

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:33.420
charged with determining

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:35.670
whether the prisoners had
been properly classified

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:37.710
as enemy combatants.

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:40.987
Depending on your perspective,
CSRTs were either quote,

00:17:40.987 --> 00:17:43.170
"The most generous set
of procedural protections

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.180
ever afforded aliens
detained by this country

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:46.650
as enemy combatants,"

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:49.500
as Chief Justice Roberts
would later write,

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.210
or a Potemkin proceeding designed

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:53.370
to offer an illusion of legal process

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:55.860
and thereby stave off
full-blown intervention

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.930
by federal courts.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:00.630
The CSRTs were composed of a panel

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:02.700
of three military officers and an officer

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:06.930
known as the recorder who served
in the prosecutorial role.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:09.690
The prisoners had no legal representation.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.640
The tribunal could rely
on secret evidence,

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:12.663
and invariably did,

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:16.860
and on evidence obtained
through coercive interrogation.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:18.360
If the CSRT determined

00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:20.010
that the prisoner was properly classified

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:21.450
as an enemy combatant,

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.563
which was the outcome in all but 38

00:18:23.563 --> 00:18:25.380
of the 558 hearings conducted

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:29.130
between July of 2004 and March of 2005,

00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:31.890
that prisoner could be held
at Guantanamo indefinitely,

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:33.750
subject only to an annual review

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:35.650
before an administrative review board.

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:40.620
Meanwhile, even as the Department
of Defense was insisting

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.230
that Guantanamo held
the worst of the worst,

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:44.490
hardened terrorists who would,

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:47.467
in the words of the nation's
top uniform military officer

00:18:47.467 --> 00:18:51.330
"chew through a hydraulics
cable to bring a plane down",

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.940
the Bush administration had
set up a separate detention

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:56.940
and interrogation regime for
a smaller number of prisoners

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:01.650
whom the CIA deemed high
value detainees, or HVDs.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:04.110
These were the so-called black sites,

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:05.430
the aptly named dungeons,

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:07.170
where the U.S. committed the gravest

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:10.710
of all human rights abuses,
forced disappearance,

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.743
incommunicado detention,
brutal torture, even homicide.

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:15.930
It was in these prisons

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:18.420
that CIA interrogators
employ the techniques

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:20.040
that were purportedly authorized

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:23.220
by the notorious Department
of Justice "torture memos",

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:25.680
including slamming prisoners into walls,

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.290
depriving them of sleep
for prolonged periods,

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:30.360
chaining them in stress positions,

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.300
placing them in a
confinement box with insects,

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:34.203
and waterboarding.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:37.950
In September of 2006,

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:40.890
the CIA's remaining high
value detainees were upgraded

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:44.520
from secret CIA detention to
indefinite military detention

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.073
and transferred from
black sites to Guantanamo.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.230
Shortly after their transfer,

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:52.830
the 14 former CIA prisoners
went through the CSRT process

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:55.500
and transcripts of those
hearings were released.

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.840
Those transcripts were incomplete.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:00.570
Virtually all references to
the prisoners' interrogation

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:03.300
and conditions of
confinement had been scrubbed

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.490
from the released versions.

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:08.820
For example, during the
March 14th, 2006 CSRT hearing

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:10.410
for Abd al-Rahim al-Nashiri,

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:14.400
Mr. Nashiri stated that he had
been tortured in CIA custody.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:15.810
When a tribunal member asked him

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:17.970
to describe the methods
that had been used,

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:20.857
the released transcript
recorded the following,

00:20:20.857 --> 00:20:24.510
"Redacted, what else do I want to say?

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:27.630
Redacted. They were doing so many things.

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:29.970
What else did they did? Redacted.

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:33.360
After that, another method
of torture began. Redacted.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:34.500
They used to ask me questions

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.240
and the investigator
after that used to laugh.

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.250
And I used to answer
the answer that I knew.

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:41.697
And if I didn't replay what I
heard, he used to, redacted."

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:46.200
All of that has been scene
setting for tonight's main event,

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:49.230
American Civil Liberties Union
versus Department of Defense

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.540
and Central Intelligence Agency,

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:54.210
the ACLU submitted a Freedom
of Information Act request

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:57.420
on April 20, 2007, seeking complete

00:20:57.420 --> 00:21:00.690
and unredacted records of
these CSRT proceedings,

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:04.560
and we followed that request
with a lawsuit to enforce it.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:07.350
We knew better than to be optimistic,

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.840
but it seemed to us that
the government's basis

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:13.890
for classifying these transcripts
was exceptionally weak.

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:17.340
It would get even weaker as
the litigation progressed.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.160
First, we weren't asking for
official government records

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.140
describing the torture program.

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:23.310
We simply wanted transcripts

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:27.240
that recorded the prisoners'
experiences in their own words.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:30.150
Unlike the notorious "torture memos",

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:31.320
the legal opinions drafted

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:33.960
by the Department of Justice's
Office of Legal Counsel

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:35.880
that purported to authorize
the brutal techniques

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:37.830
these prisoners were subjected to,

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.480
these transcripts didn't constitute

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:41.370
official government policy.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:42.854
On the contrary,

00:21:42.854 --> 00:21:44.400
they were the abbreviated
firsthand accounts

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.830
of some of our most reviled adversaries,

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:49.500
accounts that were,
therefore, easily dismissible

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:51.150
and had, in fact, been routinely dismissed

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:53.580
as lies by senior officials.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.880
Second, by the time this
lawsuit reached the courts,

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:58.923
the CIA's torture program was no secret.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:01.860
Detailed accounts of the CIA's black sites

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:04.680
and the brutality that occurred
there had been published

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.210
and widely disseminated.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:07.860
During the course of the litigation,

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:10.500
and particularly, after
President Obama took office,

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:12.000
many official records of abuse,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:13.170
including the "torture memos",

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:15.360
were declassified and released.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:19.110
By any reasonable metric, the
release of these transcripts

00:22:19.110 --> 00:22:21.600
should have cost the
government very little.

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.343
That was not the CIA's view.

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:27.240
Throughout the litigation,
the CIA insisted

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:29.010
that the redacted portions
of the transcripts

00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:32.460
must retain their "Top
Secret" designation,

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:34.920
meaning their release
reasonably could be expected

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:38.310
to cause exceptionally grave
damage to national security

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:39.570
because they included information

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:42.990
about the agency's intelligence,
sources, and methods.

00:22:42.990 --> 00:22:44.880
Before we take a deeper dive

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:47.850
into some of the absurdities
of the government's argument,

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:51.330
I want to linger for a
moment on that core claim,

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:54.450
that release of a few dozen
pages of hearing transcripts

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:56.100
in which prisoners
describe their treatment

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.923
in CIA custody would endanger the nation.

00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:02.970
Those of us who work in this
area have become so inure

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:05.430
to this casual threat
inflation that we rarely paused

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:08.100
to consider what the government
is asking us to believe.

00:23:08.100 --> 00:23:09.180
In this case, that the release

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:12.300
of these 40 or so pages
would have any impact

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:14.340
on the security of a global hyperpower,

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.563
much less an exceptionally grave one.

00:23:17.580 --> 00:23:20.670
Such an assertion should
strike any reasonable observer

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:22.320
as fundamentally odd,

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:25.290
and the government offered
some very odd arguments

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:27.060
in its defense.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:28.080
The first of those arguments

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:30.237
was that the government had the authority

00:23:30.237 --> 00:23:32.287
to classify these transcripts
in the first place.

00:23:32.287 --> 00:23:34.807
"By what law or logic," we wondered,

00:23:34.807 --> 00:23:36.030
"could the government classify

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.400
the prisoner's own words and memories

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:41.487
derive from their own
observations and experiences?"

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:43.260
The government's authority

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:45.150
to detain a prisoner had
never been understood

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:47.190
to encompass the authority to classify any

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:49.293
and all utterances made by that prisoner.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:51.240
And the legal requirement

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:54.150
that classified national
security information

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:55.860
be owned by, produced by, or for,

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.410
or be under the control of
the United States government

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:01.590
had never before been
extended to human beings

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.810
under the government's control.

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:07.380
Put even more bluntly, the CIA was arguing

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:08.970
that it can subject an individual

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:11.550
to enhanced interrogation techniques,

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:14.940
and then by virtue of that
individual's involuntary exposure

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:16.470
to those techniques alone,

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:19.323
enforce a permanent gag
on his communication.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.290
In a sworn declaration
submitted to the court,

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:25.500
a CIA Associate Information Officer stated

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:27.570
that the 14 high value detainees included

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:30.030
in plaintiff's request "have been exposed"

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:32.430
to intelligence activities, sources,

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:34.830
and methods of information.

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.890
Specifically quoting, "The
intelligence activities

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:39.960
to which the high value
detainees were exposed

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:43.050
include the capture,
detention, confinement,

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:45.327
and interrogation of detainees."

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:50.013
That is, they were exposed,
as it were, to themselves.

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:54.630
What's more, the CIA's ability
to conceal this information

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:57.390
was predicated entirely on the
government's physical custody

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:58.770
of the detainees.

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:00.810
In other words, the argument presumed

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:02.520
that they would not be released.

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:05.340
By this logic, the ACLU argued,

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:07.350
the CIA could mistakenly detain

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:10.560
and coercively interrogate
an entirely innocent person,

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.260
something that in fact
happened many times.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:14.880
But if that person were exposed

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:17.070
to classified interrogation techniques,

00:25:17.070 --> 00:25:19.410
permitting him to describe his ordeal

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:22.350
would cause exceptionally grave
harm to national security,

00:25:22.350 --> 00:25:25.470
so his release would be impossible.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:28.230
Of course, if the government were correct

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:29.670
that the detainee's exposure

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:32.220
to classified intelligence,
sources, and methods

00:25:32.220 --> 00:25:34.833
justified an indefinite
gag on their communication,

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:36.810
then surely it would follow

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:38.370
that whoever disclosed those methods

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.370
to terrorism suspects had
violated criminal statutes,

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:43.410
prohibiting the transmission
of intelligence secrets

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:46.500
to anyone unauthorized to receive them.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:48.630
That is to say, at the very moment,

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:51.390
that hooded CIA interrogators
were pouring water

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:53.520
down the throat of Khalid Sheikh Mohammed,

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:55.650
they were engaged in an unauthorized leak

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:57.960
of top secret information
to an avowed enemy

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.180
of the United States, no less.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:01.503
That's obviously silly.

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:04.950
The CIA interrogators and their
bosses should be prosecuted

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:06.243
for torture, not leaking.

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:10.230
But is it any sillier than the
government's argument here?

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:12.030
And that was not even the strangest

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:14.520
of the arguments that the government made.

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.740
The government had
repeatedly characterized

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:20.430
the detainees' torture
allegations as false.

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:22.620
Secretary of Defense
Rumsfeld, for example,

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:24.540
responded to a question about torture

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:26.557
at Guantanamo with ridicule,

00:26:26.557 --> 00:26:28.140
"The detainees were doing exactly

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:30.070
what they were trained to tell people.

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:32.940
'Tell them you're tortured.
Tell them it's terrible.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:35.280
Tell them this. Tell them that.'

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:36.330
That's what they do."

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.900
Even if we were to accept the argument

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.570
that the detainees true
accounts of their own detention

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:46.170
and interrogation might
include sensitive details,

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:50.070
how could false accusations
disclose secrets?

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:51.900
How could the government deny

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:55.230
and classify the same allegations?

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:57.360
Easily, as it turns out,

00:26:57.360 --> 00:26:59.820
and with no discernible embarrassment.

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:02.070
According to the CIA sworn declaration,

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:04.620
even false allegations
about the classified details

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:07.830
of the program must be treated
as classified information.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:09.780
The CIA did not purport to explain

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:12.480
how an allegation could
simultaneously be false

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:15.870
and be about the classified
details of the program,

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:18.093
an assertion that defies ordinary logic.

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:22.140
The declaration then
offered a hypothetical.

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.510
If the government were to
redact only true allegations

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:27.540
regarding locations of
CIA detention facilities,

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:29.770
then the true locations
of these facilities

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.240
could be revealed by
making multiple allegations

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:37.560
as to location through a
simple process of elimination.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:39.900
That doesn't strike me as very simple.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:41.820
Was the CIA suggesting that publication

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:44.610
of a detainee's false
assertion that he was detained

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:48.000
in, say, Afghanistan, could
alert a canny observer

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:50.100
that the true location was Morocco

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:53.010
through some kind of
terrorist decoder ring?

00:27:53.010 --> 00:27:54.690
Or was the government imagining a scenario

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.760
in which an expert
manipulator gamed the system

00:27:56.760 --> 00:28:00.243
by falsely naming every nation
in the world except one?

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:02.943
The CIA did not elaborate.

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:06.030
While the ACLU and the government

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.800
were trading sharply-worded briefs,

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:09.810
President Obama took office.

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:11.940
And in one of his first executive orders,

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:15.000
he categorically banned the
interrogation techniques

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.360
that the CIA insisted were
classified sources and methods

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:20.010
of intelligence gathering.

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:22.890
This should have resolved the litigation.

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:25.080
How could a banned technique,

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:29.070
a technique that the CIA
by law could not use again,

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:31.653
remain a classifiable source and method?

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:34.350
Even assuming that the
torture techniques were

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.270
at one point legitimate and lawful methods

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:37.830
of intelligence gathering,

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:39.000
no amount of disclosure

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.010
about their past use could reveal details

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:43.350
about current intelligence
sources and methods,

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:45.873
then might be properly
protected by the CIA.

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:50.250
In a new sworn declaration,
the CIA acknowledged

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:52.440
that the interrogation techniques at issue

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:55.957
had been prohibited by law, but it argued,

00:28:55.957 --> 00:28:58.050
"Even if the enhanced
interrogation techniques

00:28:58.050 --> 00:28:59.490
are never used again,

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:02.010
the United States will
continue to be involved

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:05.100
in questioning terrorists under
legally approved guidelines.

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:06.660
The information in these documents

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:09.000
would provide future
terrorists with a guidebook

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:10.767
on how to evade such questioning."

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:14.250
If you're wondering exactly how knowledge

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:16.380
about banned interrogation techniques

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:17.850
would aid future captives

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:21.303
in resisting new and different
techniques, you're not alone.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:24.723
The CIA made no effort to explain that.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:28.470
More disorienting, was
that the CIA continued

00:29:28.470 --> 00:29:31.290
to press arguments in court
that directly contradicted

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:35.490
what the President, their
putative boss, had said in public.

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:37.050
President Obama had repudiated

00:29:37.050 --> 00:29:39.540
the CIA's brutal interrogation techniques,

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:41.157
and stated that they
did not advance our war

00:29:41.157 --> 00:29:43.230
and counter-terrorism efforts.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:44.230
They undermine them.

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:47.250
But the government continued
to argue in our litigation

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:48.247
that those techniques had quote,

00:29:48.247 --> 00:29:49.920
"Provided the U.S. government

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:51.510
with one of the most useful tools

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:54.000
in combating terrorist
threats to national security."

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:55.350
The CIA told the court

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:57.573
that the now banned techniques had quote,

00:29:57.573 --> 00:29:58.920
"Led to the disruption
of a plot to fly a plane

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:01.200
into the tallest building in Los Angeles

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:03.150
and a plot to hijack passenger planes

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:05.250
to fly into Heathrow Airport,"

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:07.800
even though those claims
had been repeatedly debunked

00:30:07.800 --> 00:30:10.110
and were provably false.

00:30:10.110 --> 00:30:11.880
The Bush White House
had, itself, confirmed

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:13.920
that the Los Angeles plot was disrupted

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.330
before the CIA's detention

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:17.670
and interrogation program even began,

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:19.830
and the British government
dismissed the CIA's claim

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:23.970
about the Heathrow plot as
completely and utterly wrong.

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:27.487
In a 2010 op-ed, Boris Johnson wrote that,

00:30:27.487 --> 00:30:29.460
"The CIA's torture of
prisoners had yielded

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:31.890
no information of value to the British,"

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:34.560
and that, "if former President
Bush traveled to the U.K.,

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:36.930
he might fight face arrest for the crime

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.887
of waterboarding prisoners and
would never see Texas again."

00:30:42.060 --> 00:30:43.500
To my knowledge, President Bush

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:46.200
and Vice President Cheney
have gone nowhere near Europe

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:47.373
since leaving office.

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:52.080
There was yet another dispositive reason

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:55.860
why withholding these transcripts
was legally impermissible.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:58.110
By the time the courts
were considering the case,

00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:00.360
the government had
declassified numerous documents

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:03.330
that formally acknowledged
and described in detail

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:05.580
the interrogation
techniques and conditions

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:08.010
of confinement at issue in the litigation.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:10.470
I've already mentioned the
notorious "torture memos",

00:31:10.470 --> 00:31:14.190
which President Obama
declassified in April of 2009.

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:17.610
These contained concrete and
startling operational details,

00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:19.560
including the angle of the gurney

00:31:19.560 --> 00:31:22.710
and the temperature of the
water used in waterboarding,

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:25.623
the protocols for stress
positions and sleep deprivation,

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.980
and the appalling admission

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:30.780
that one prisoner had
been deprived of sleep

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:33.783
for 180 hours, more than a week.

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:36.053
A few months later,

00:31:36.053 --> 00:31:38.700
the Obama administration also
declassified large portions

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:42.180
of a CIA Inspector
General report from 2004.

00:31:42.180 --> 00:31:44.070
That report described multiple instances

00:31:44.070 --> 00:31:46.200
in which CIA interrogators exceeded

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:49.290
the authority purportedly
granted by the "torture memos",

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:51.750
including incidents in which
al-Nashiri was threatened

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:55.440
with a semi-automatic
handgun and a power drill,

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:57.870
and Khalid Sheikh Mohammed
was warned by interrogators

00:31:57.870 --> 00:31:59.730
that his children would be killed

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:02.230
if there were another
terrorist attack in the U.S.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:05.970
In contrast to the transcripts
at issue in our litigation,

00:32:05.970 --> 00:32:08.970
which simply recorded the
detainee's accounts of torture,

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.490
these were official government records.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:13.890
This presented a new twist.

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:17.340
The government had, for
decades, argued successfully

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:19.440
that publication of mere allegations

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:23.130
about classified activities
could not compel the disclosure

00:32:23.130 --> 00:32:25.290
of official government documents.

00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:28.140
Here, apparently for the first time,

00:32:28.140 --> 00:32:30.960
it argued that publication of
official government documents

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:34.170
about classified activities
could not compel the disclosure

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:35.970
of mere allegations.

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:39.330
Heads, we win. Tails, you lose.

00:32:39.330 --> 00:32:42.780
So to review, the transcripts
contain descriptions

00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:44.400
of the detainee's own experiences,

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:46.500
not any official government account.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:49.350
The details of their interrogations
had been declassified

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:51.390
and made public in numerous ways.

00:32:51.390 --> 00:32:52.980
The President had banned the techniques

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.350
that were being withheld as
classified sources and methods.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:59.640
And despite all of that,
the CIA still swore

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:01.950
and the Department of Justice still argued

00:33:01.950 --> 00:33:03.330
that the release of these transcripts

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:05.460
would cause exceptionally grave damage

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:08.130
to the security of the United States.

00:33:08.130 --> 00:33:11.280
By this point, I doubt
anyone is in suspense

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:13.500
about what happened next.

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:16.020
The federal district
court in Washington, D.C.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:18.420
had little difficulty
sweeping our arguments aside

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:20.310
in a cursory order.

00:33:20.310 --> 00:33:22.680
It upheld the government's
suppression of the transcripts

00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:26.130
without even conducting
in-camera review to examine them.

00:33:26.130 --> 00:33:30.870
In other words, it deemed the
CIA's affidavits so credible

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:33.060
that it declined even to
look at the transcripts

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:35.793
before awarding summary
judgment to the government.

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:38.940
We appealed and the court of appeals

00:33:38.940 --> 00:33:41.310
for the District of Columbia
Circuit held oral arguments

00:33:41.310 --> 00:33:42.993
in September of 2010.

00:33:43.830 --> 00:33:47.370
The appellate panel
included conservative icons

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:49.650
Laurence Silberman and David Sentelle,

00:33:49.650 --> 00:33:53.223
who made no effort to hide
their disdain for the lawsuit.

00:33:54.090 --> 00:33:55.230
They began by asking me

00:33:55.230 --> 00:33:58.230
why the ACLU even wanted the transcripts.

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:00.660
If the interrogation details
had been released elsewhere,

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:03.120
what was the point of our request?"

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:04.897
As Judge Silberman put it,

00:34:04.897 --> 00:34:06.580
"The argument is not very
convincing that says,

00:34:06.580 --> 00:34:09.480
'They should let us have this
because we already have it.'

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:11.250
If we have a non-sequitur contest,

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:12.777
you get to enter that one."

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:17.220
I responded that, "Under the
binding law of their circuit,

00:34:17.220 --> 00:34:19.380
the ACLU would be
entitled to the documents

00:34:19.380 --> 00:34:22.020
only if the information the CIA claimed

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:25.050
as sources and methods had
been released elsewhere.

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:27.840
So any non-sequitur resulted
from their precedence,

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:29.227
not my illogic."

00:34:29.227 --> 00:34:31.267
"In any event," I argued,

00:34:31.267 --> 00:34:32.910
"under the FOIA, it was not my burden

00:34:32.910 --> 00:34:35.220
to explain why I wanted the documents.

00:34:35.220 --> 00:34:36.390
It was the government's burden

00:34:36.390 --> 00:34:38.677
to justify their withholding."

00:34:38.677 --> 00:34:41.407
"Wait a minute," said Justice Silberman.

00:34:41.407 --> 00:34:43.380
"If this information is
already in the public record,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:46.680
how do you even have standing
to sue for it under the FOIA?

00:34:46.680 --> 00:34:49.440
How is there even a case or controversy?"

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:50.910
This was another new one,

00:34:50.910 --> 00:34:53.910
and really the definition of a catch-22.

00:34:53.910 --> 00:34:56.040
To overcome the government's
claimed exemptions,

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:57.210
we were required to argue

00:34:57.210 --> 00:34:58.890
that virtually identical information

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:00.480
had already been disclosed.

00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:02.640
But if we argue that virtually
identical information

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:03.690
had already been disclosed,

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:05.670
we would have no standing to sue.

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:08.880
Heads, we win. Tails, you lose.

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:10.590
Judge Silberman's next line of questioning

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:12.307
threw me for a loop,

00:35:12.307 --> 00:35:14.610
"Did you ever see the movie
'Saving Private Ryan'?"

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:16.447
he asked me.
(audience chuckling)

00:35:16.447 --> 00:35:18.397
"I've seen it, your honor."

00:35:18.397 --> 00:35:20.040
"Do you remember the scene on Omaha Beach

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:22.320
when German soldiers were
trying to surrender to GIs,

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:24.120
but they were shot?"

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.270
I remembered the scene.

00:35:26.377 --> 00:35:28.350
"If there had been a FOIA at that time,

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:29.700
and the shootings had been recorded,

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:31.710
and someone had requested the videos,

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:34.890
would the government have had
to release them in wartime?"

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:37.680
And here, you have to
admire the casual equation

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:40.470
of wartime Normandy in the spring of 1944

00:35:40.470 --> 00:35:41.700
with wartime Washington

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:45.600
and that fraught and
perilous autumn of 2010.

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:48.120
I responded, "It's our view

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:50.670
that the government cannot use a crime

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:52.620
as the basis for a coverup.

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:54.540
A contrary rule would create
a perverse incentive,"

00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:55.373
I argued.

00:35:55.373 --> 00:35:56.640
"The worse the government's conduct,

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:58.950
the more exempt it would
be from disclosure.

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:01.290
This was the opposite of
what Congress intended

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.240
when it enacted the FOIA."

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:05.370
I'm cherry picking some of my better lines

00:36:05.370 --> 00:36:07.950
in this retelling, but I
should acknowledge here

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:10.980
that the court somewhat
sadistically posts audio recordings

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:13.200
of oral arguments on its website.

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:16.950
And the recording of this one
paints a different picture.

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.680
The judges are relaxed, almost bored,

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:22.740
enjoying some occasional
sport at my expense.

00:36:22.740 --> 00:36:25.470
I'm testy and can't hide my annoyance.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:27.020
We all know how it's gonna end.

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:31.110
In an opinion issued on January 18, 2011,

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:33.330
the court unanimously
affirmed summary judgment

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:36.360
for the government on the
basis of the CIA's insistence

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:38.070
that release of the unredacted transcripts

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:40.440
would damage the security
of the United States.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.660
It did so, as had the district court,

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:45.300
without even taking a
few minutes to glance

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:46.800
at the disputed documents

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:48.843
to see if the CIA was lying about them.

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:55.410
I've spent more time than I
should have on this particular,

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:59.070
and ultimately, minor case
because I think it's possible

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:01.230
to distill from it the core tenets

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:03.273
of national security law and practice.

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:05.463
The government wins.

00:37:06.630 --> 00:37:08.250
Almost nonsensical arguments

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:09.600
that would insult the intelligence

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:11.347
of a junior high school debater,

00:37:11.347 --> 00:37:14.700
"You are prohibited from
speaking about these things

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:17.793
that never happened," are
put forth in somber tones.

00:37:18.629 --> 00:37:22.110
The government demands that
judges defer to the expertise

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:25.710
of the very officials who stand
accused of grave misconduct.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:28.890
That demand is accompanied by
wildly inflated claims of harm

00:37:28.890 --> 00:37:32.070
that will result if the court
were, instead, to do its job.

00:37:32.070 --> 00:37:34.800
And finally, the ostensible
subject of the dispute,

00:37:34.800 --> 00:37:37.890
in this case, transparency versus secrecy,

00:37:37.890 --> 00:37:40.800
is a contrived proxy for
what's actually at stake,

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:43.950
accountability versus impunity.

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:46.980
It should be obvious that the
CIA's concern was not harm

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:49.650
to the nation, but harm to the CIA.

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:51.780
The audience they had in
mind was not terrorists

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:54.660
who might learn to evade their
interrogation techniques,

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:58.110
but the American people who
might be appalled by them.

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:00.360
The lesson of Abu Ghraib was fresh.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.100
Images of torture could produce

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:04.380
a dramatically different public reaction

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:06.120
than written descriptions.

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:08.400
This explains the decision
of senior CIA leaders

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:11.430
to destroy video evidence
of torture in black sites.

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:13.920
The agency was willing to
risk potential liability

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:15.510
for obstruction of justice

00:38:15.510 --> 00:38:18.120
to avoid the more probable
fallout that would occur

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:21.150
if the horrific videos
were seen and disseminated.

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:23.520
Might the public react strongly

00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:25.260
to the emotional firsthand accounts

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:28.320
of human suffering contained
in these redacted transcripts?

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:31.380
The CIA didn't want to take the chance,

00:38:31.380 --> 00:38:32.480
and it didn't have to.

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:35.340
We would later learn a
more significant reason

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:37.350
for this suppression of the transcripts.

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:38.730
They contained compelling evidence

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:41.763
that the CIA's torture
regime had been ineffective.

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:44.430
And of course, this was precisely

00:38:44.430 --> 00:38:46.650
why we wanted these documents.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:49.650
President Obama had banned
torture by executive order,

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:52.050
but there was nothing to
bind a future president

00:38:53.465 --> 00:38:54.298
intent on reinstating it.

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:56.580
Meanwhile, all of our lawsuits

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:58.950
on behalf of torture
victims had been dismissed

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:00.660
on justiciability grounds

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:04.230
with no ruling on the
legality of the practices.

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:06.090
And while President
Obama had disappointed us

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:08.670
with his insistence that a
nation could look forward

00:39:08.670 --> 00:39:11.100
without looking backwards,
we clung to the hope

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:13.500
that we could generate
enough public backlash

00:39:13.500 --> 00:39:16.050
to reinvigorate demands
for accountability.

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:17.250
At a minimum,

00:39:17.250 --> 00:39:20.040
we hoped to assemble a
comprehensive public record

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:21.900
that might be of value to investigators

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.003
in another place or time.

00:39:26.370 --> 00:39:27.930
I made the claim a minute ago

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:29.460
that in national security litigation,

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:32.610
disputes over secrecy operate as a proxy

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:34.860
for disputes over accountability.

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:37.610
I want to illustrate more
directly what I mean by that.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:41.280
Simply put, in 2023 as we stand here,

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:44.730
very little is secret about
the CIA's torture program.

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:47.430
But as a consequence of
legal secrecy doctrines,

00:39:47.430 --> 00:39:51.180
every single lawsuit filed by
a torture victim since 9/11

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:54.483
has ended without any
adjudication of the law or facts.

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:56.550
In layman's terms,

00:39:56.550 --> 00:39:58.800
no court that was faced
with a torture lawsuit

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:00.480
ever addressed the core question

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:03.630
of whether the victim's
rights were violated.

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:06.510
This includes the case of Khalid El-Masri,

00:40:06.510 --> 00:40:08.490
maybe the most well-known victim

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:12.513
of the CIA's so-called
extraordinary rendition program.

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:15.840
In a notorious case of mistaken identity,

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:17.880
El-Masri, a German citizen,

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:20.700
was abducted by Macedonian
security officers

00:40:20.700 --> 00:40:22.230
while visiting that country,

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:24.840
detained incommunicado for weeks,

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:26.940
handed over to a CIA renditions team,

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:30.690
then stripped, beaten,
drugged via anal suppository,

00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:32.820
diapered, chained to the floor of a plane,

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:36.120
and transported to a secret
CIA prison in Afghanistan

00:40:36.120 --> 00:40:38.580
where he was detained
in abusive conditions

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:40.260
for several months.

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:42.810
Senior U.S. officials
realized very quickly

00:40:42.810 --> 00:40:45.210
that they had kidnapped
an innocent person.

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:46.650
Because he was a European citizen,

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:49.260
he couldn't be dropped in
Guantanamo to be sorted out later.

00:40:49.260 --> 00:40:52.380
So the CIA worked with German
intelligence to devise a plan

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:54.060
that would return him to Germany

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:56.760
without leaving obvious U.S. fingerprints.

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:59.220
El-Masri was warned not
to reveal his experiences

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:01.980
to the media or to local German officials.

00:41:01.980 --> 00:41:03.750
Then he was flown to Albania,

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:06.120
driven blindfolded to a remote location,

00:41:06.120 --> 00:41:07.980
and left on the side of the road,

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:09.540
whereby prearrangement, he was picked up

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.333
by Albanian officers
and put on a plane home.

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:15.690
This poorly scripted attempt

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:19.710
to avoid international
scandal flopped badly.

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:21.270
On his return to Germany,

00:41:21.270 --> 00:41:23.670
El-Masri promptly contacted an attorney

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:26.160
who reported his story
to the German police.

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:29.190
Public prosecutors initiated
a formal investigation

00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:30.960
that would later result in arrest warrants

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:33.960
for 13 suspected CIA agents.

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:37.980
Other European investigations
surfaced every damning detail

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:39.900
of this ugly episode.

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:42.660
Macedonia was ordered by a European court

00:41:42.660 --> 00:41:45.300
to pay compensation to El-Masri.

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.580
I won't offer a full anatomy

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:49.530
of the ACLU'S lawsuit that followed,

00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:52.590
but you heard the thumbnail
version in my introduction.

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:55.560
Our suit against CIA officials
and contractors was dismissed

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:57.210
at the outset on the basis

00:41:57.210 --> 00:41:59.520
of an affidavit submitted by, who else?

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:02.550
The CIA director, who
insisted that any litigation

00:42:02.550 --> 00:42:05.790
of El-Masri's claims would
disclose state secrets

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:09.120
and gravely damage the nation's security.

00:42:09.120 --> 00:42:10.290
As we would later argue

00:42:10.290 --> 00:42:13.260
in an unsuccessful Supreme Court petition,

00:42:13.260 --> 00:42:16.020
the El-Masri case now
stands for the proposition

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:18.600
that the government can engage in torture,

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:20.400
declare it a state secret,

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:22.620
and by virtue of that designation alone,

00:42:22.620 --> 00:42:24.780
avoid any accountability for conduct

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:27.333
that has long been unlawful
in all circumstances.

00:42:28.170 --> 00:42:31.080
What's so unsettling about the
secrecy claims in this case

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:32.700
is that El-Masri was not locked away

00:42:32.700 --> 00:42:34.230
in a black site or Guantanamo.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:36.420
He was free and available to testify,

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:38.280
and we were prepared
to litigate his claims

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:40.140
on public evidence.

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:42.180
In fact, he was sitting in the courtroom

00:42:42.180 --> 00:42:44.970
in Richmond, Virginia when
Department of Justice argued

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:46.530
to the panel of appellate judges

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:47.670
that the entire subject matter

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:49.897
of his lawsuit was a state secret.

00:42:49.897 --> 00:42:52.890
"I am not a state secret,"
El-Masri would later write

00:42:52.890 --> 00:42:55.680
in an op-ed piece in
the Los Angeles Times.

00:42:55.680 --> 00:42:58.890
As we'll discuss in more detail tomorrow,

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:00.960
the government prevails in these cases

00:43:00.960 --> 00:43:03.030
by insisting on a distinction

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:05.130
between what the entire world knows

00:43:05.130 --> 00:43:08.400
and what the government itself
is prepared to acknowledge.

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:11.340
In El-Masri's case, that
meant that his own testimony,

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.770
as well as all of the evidence
on earth by investigators,

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:16.320
all of the witnesses, lab
tests, public records,

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:18.450
even the judgements of foreign courts,

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:21.420
amounted to zero so
long as the CIA refused

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:22.923
to make a formal confession.

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:26.430
This gives the executive
branch complete control

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:30.780
over its own liability, even
for the most serious crimes.

00:43:30.780 --> 00:43:33.780
There are a lot of words to
describe what's going on here,

00:43:33.780 --> 00:43:35.370
but I would submit that secrecy

00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:37.083
isn't a particularly apt one.

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:42.600
In June of 2016, more than five years

00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:45.990
after the Court of Appeals
upheld the CIA suppression

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:47.850
of the Guantanamo transcripts,

00:43:47.850 --> 00:43:51.390
the government quietly released
nearly complete versions

00:43:51.390 --> 00:43:54.063
in response to a new ACLU FOIA lawsuit.

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:56.130
By that time, it was clear,

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:57.690
if there had been any lingering doubt,

00:43:57.690 --> 00:44:00.600
that the Obama administration
had absolutely no interest

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:02.670
In pursuing further accountability

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:04.770
for the torture conspiracy.

00:44:04.770 --> 00:44:08.193
The unredacted transcripts
make for harrowing reading.

00:44:09.127 --> 00:44:11.160
"They pulled my hands up and cuffed them,"

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:13.530
recounts Majid Khan, "so I
won't be able to kneel down

00:44:13.530 --> 00:44:15.960
and get rest for three days.

00:44:15.960 --> 00:44:17.160
And when they took my clothes off,

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:19.980
and took me to the bathroom,
and dipped me in full ice,

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:22.200
an ice water tub, and
they hang me back again.

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:24.330
And this time, they
would throw cold water.

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:25.950
And every few hours, and right next to me,

00:44:25.950 --> 00:44:27.210
they would turn on a fan.

00:44:27.210 --> 00:44:30.367
Days passed like this,
three straight days."

00:44:30.367 --> 00:44:32.550
"They shackle me
completely, even my head,"

00:44:32.550 --> 00:44:33.937
recalls Abu Zubaydah.

00:44:33.937 --> 00:44:35.970
"I can't do anything, like this.

00:44:35.970 --> 00:44:37.890
And they put one cloth in my mouth,

00:44:37.890 --> 00:44:40.470
and they put water, water, water.

00:44:40.470 --> 00:44:43.170
Last point before I
die, they stand the bed,

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:45.670
and I tell him, 'If you
want to kill me, kill me.'

00:44:46.710 --> 00:44:48.870
But it turned out that
brutality wasn't all

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:51.390
that the CIA had hoped to conceal.

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:53.070
Several prisoners testified

00:44:53.070 --> 00:44:55.080
that they had made false confessions

00:44:55.080 --> 00:44:57.960
and concocted fake terrorist
plots under torture

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:00.150
to appease their interrogators.

00:45:00.150 --> 00:45:02.280
Abu Zubaydah requested numerous times

00:45:02.280 --> 00:45:05.880
that the CIA produce for the
tribunal an email he had sent

00:45:05.880 --> 00:45:09.450
to Islamic scholars after
9/11, asking them to clarify

00:45:09.450 --> 00:45:12.810
whether killing innocent
civilians was permissible,

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:15.390
suggesting not only
that he wasn't involved,

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:17.490
but that he hadn't approved.

00:45:17.490 --> 00:45:19.890
And he testified that a CIA
officer had acknowledged

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:21.697
that he wasn't even a member
of Al Qaeda and said to him,

00:45:21.697 --> 00:45:24.147
"Sorry, we made a big mistake."

00:45:25.050 --> 00:45:28.590
This is what the CIA had
fought so hard to conceal

00:45:28.590 --> 00:45:30.990
under the banner of sources and methods.

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:33.750
The dirtiest secret was
that the torture prisons

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:36.513
were not just sadistic,
they were gratuitous.

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:40.083
The story has one more coda.

00:45:41.370 --> 00:45:44.400
Even as civil and
criminal liability efforts

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:46.170
were being extinguished in the U.S.,

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:48.030
investigations proceeded overseas,

00:45:48.030 --> 00:45:51.090
including in Poland where
Abu Zubaydah had been held

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:55.549
in a CIA black site in 2002 and 2003.

00:45:55.549 --> 00:45:57.810
In 2010, Zubaydah's American lawyers

00:45:57.810 --> 00:45:59.820
filed a criminal complaint in Poland

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:01.770
asking prosecutors there to investigate

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:03.720
whether any Polish nationals were involved

00:46:03.720 --> 00:46:04.870
in his harsh treatment.

00:46:05.820 --> 00:46:08.250
Polish prosecutors reached out
to the U.S. for information,

00:46:08.250 --> 00:46:10.410
but were rebuffed on
national security grounds,

00:46:10.410 --> 00:46:12.110
so they close their investigation.

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:15.960
But in 2015, the European
Court for Human Rights

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:18.240
took up the case and concluded
beyond a reasonable doubt,

00:46:18.240 --> 00:46:19.713
based on voluminous evidence,

00:46:20.610 --> 00:46:22.710
that Zubaydah had in fact
been held and tortured

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:25.470
at a CIA prison in Poland,
and that Poland had failed

00:46:25.470 --> 00:46:27.510
to investigate the matter adequately.

00:46:27.510 --> 00:46:30.690
Polish prosecutors again sought
information from the U.S.,

00:46:30.690 --> 00:46:32.790
and they were again rebuffed.

00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:34.770
The prosecutors then
told Zubaydah's lawyers,

00:46:34.770 --> 00:46:36.450
they were free to submit any evidence

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.130
that might aid the investigation,

00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:41.100
and Zubaydah's lawyers
served discovery requests

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:42.900
on two CIA psychologists

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:44.900
who had worked in the CIA's black sites.

00:46:46.380 --> 00:46:47.970
The CIA moved to block these efforts,

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:50.100
invoking the state secrets privilege.

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:51.210
While it was true,

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:55.020
CIA director Mike Pompeo
conceded in a sworn affidavit,

00:46:55.020 --> 00:46:55.950
that many details

00:46:55.950 --> 00:46:58.230
of the CIA's enhanced
interrogation program

00:46:58.230 --> 00:47:00.210
had now been declassified,

00:47:00.210 --> 00:47:02.910
the CIA had never formally confirmed

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:05.163
that it had operated a prison in Poland.

00:47:06.480 --> 00:47:08.550
This dispute led to what has to have been

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:10.410
one of the more bizarre
Supreme Court cases

00:47:10.410 --> 00:47:12.120
in recent memory.

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:14.190
That the CIA had run a
black site in Poland,

00:47:14.190 --> 00:47:15.810
again, was no secret.

00:47:15.810 --> 00:47:17.670
After leaving office,
the President of Poland

00:47:17.670 --> 00:47:19.500
at the time had publicly confirmed

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:22.293
that the CIA had set up the
prison with his knowledge.

00:47:23.670 --> 00:47:25.890
In what has to be considered
the ultimate demonstration

00:47:25.890 --> 00:47:28.830
of the Streisand Effect, the
Supreme Court heard arguments

00:47:28.830 --> 00:47:30.990
and published 80 pages of opinions,

00:47:30.990 --> 00:47:33.390
all devoted to the question
whether Poland's cooperation

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:36.603
with the CIA was a
legitimate state secret.

00:47:37.530 --> 00:47:41.670
In the end, seven of the nine
justices said that it was.

00:47:41.670 --> 00:47:45.567
As Ian Shapiro might say,
"Imagine the world is flat."

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:49.410
The court accepted the argument
that formal confirmation

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:52.530
of Poland's role might complicate
the CIA's future efforts

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:54.270
to enter into clandestine partnerships

00:47:54.270 --> 00:47:56.430
with foreign intelligence services.

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:58.350
This isn't illogical.

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:00.030
It's just limitless.

00:48:00.030 --> 00:48:01.470
It means that if the CIA makes sure

00:48:01.470 --> 00:48:03.330
to involve a foreign partner,

00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:05.010
it can always invoke state secrets

00:48:05.010 --> 00:48:06.483
to foreclose accountability.

00:48:07.620 --> 00:48:09.030
During the litigation,

00:48:09.030 --> 00:48:12.067
the U.S. government proposed
an alternative way forward,

00:48:12.067 --> 00:48:14.940
"Perhaps, instead of deposing
the CIA psychologists,

00:48:14.940 --> 00:48:17.310
Abu Zubaydah could write
a document from his cell

00:48:17.310 --> 00:48:20.430
in Guantanamo describing
his own recollections."

00:48:20.430 --> 00:48:22.140
This caught my attention.

00:48:22.140 --> 00:48:24.878
Was the government suggesting
as a resolution to this case,

00:48:24.878 --> 00:48:28.980
exactly what it had fought
for years to prevent in mine?

00:48:28.980 --> 00:48:29.813
Not quite.

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:31.740
The government reserved the right

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:35.490
to conduct a security review
of Zubaydah's manuscript.

00:48:35.490 --> 00:48:38.587
As Justice Gorsuch wrote
in a surprising dissent,

00:48:38.587 --> 00:48:41.220
"This was little more than
an offer to let Zubaydah say

00:48:41.220 --> 00:48:44.677
whatever the government
chooses to allow him to say."

00:48:44.677 --> 00:48:47.557
"There comes a point,"
Justice Gorsuch wrote,

00:48:47.557 --> 00:48:49.710
"where we should not be ignorant as judges

00:48:49.710 --> 00:48:53.130
of what we know to be true as citizens."

00:48:53.130 --> 00:48:55.260
For the majority of the Supreme Court,

00:48:55.260 --> 00:48:57.720
and for the U.S. legal system as a whole,

00:48:57.720 --> 00:49:00.213
that time has not yet arrived.

00:49:01.260 --> 00:49:02.580
Thank you for listening.

00:49:02.580 --> 00:49:04.530
Hope to see some of you tomorrow,

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:06.540
and I would be glad to answer questions.

00:49:06.540 --> 00:49:08.377
I should warn you the
answer to every question is,

00:49:08.377 --> 00:49:10.264
"That's in tomorrow's lecture."

00:49:10.264 --> 00:49:11.700
(audience laughing)
(Ben chuckling)

00:49:11.700 --> 00:49:14.867
(audience applauding)

00:49:19.530 --> 00:49:21.330
- [Ana] Thank you so much, Ben,

00:49:21.330 --> 00:49:25.590
for, really, that fascinating
and thought-provoking talk.

00:49:25.590 --> 00:49:27.120
We're going to use a microphone

00:49:27.120 --> 00:49:29.370
because we are recording this lecture.

00:49:29.370 --> 00:49:31.290
So if you want to pose a question,

00:49:31.290 --> 00:49:35.968
please raise your hand and I'll
bring the microphone to you.

00:49:35.968 --> 00:49:36.917
- [Audience Member 1] You
mentioned that the ACLU,

00:49:36.917 --> 00:49:40.830
one of the things you try to
do, is build public backlash.

00:49:40.830 --> 00:49:45.830
How did you go about doing
that, especially when support

00:49:46.980 --> 00:49:49.623
for the terrorist may
not have been very high?

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:52.200
- So it's a good question.

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:55.260
So when you bring litigation

00:49:55.260 --> 00:49:58.920
that you expect is not going
to succeed in a court of law,

00:49:58.920 --> 00:50:00.450
you do so with the awareness

00:50:00.450 --> 00:50:03.960
that the court of public opinion
might be the actual venue

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:05.642
that you're litigating in.

00:50:05.642 --> 00:50:06.840
So the first thing that
you need to do, of course,

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:09.760
is explain to the
plaintiffs in these cases

00:50:11.160 --> 00:50:12.990
that we're not likely to prevail,

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.420
so they don't get re-traumatized
through the process.

00:50:15.420 --> 00:50:19.260
But part of it also is,
of strategic litigation,

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:21.000
is choosing plaintiffs, right?

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:23.070
We brought a case on
behalf of Khalid El-Masri

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:25.230
who was an innocent victim.

00:50:25.230 --> 00:50:26.660
We didn't bring that
same case on behalf of,

00:50:26.660 --> 00:50:28.530
of Khalid Sheik Mohammed,

00:50:28.530 --> 00:50:30.750
who was subjected to
more brutal techniques

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:33.060
for precisely the reason you identify.

00:50:33.060 --> 00:50:34.927
If the question before the public is,

00:50:34.927 --> 00:50:37.500
"What rights should terrorists have?"

00:50:37.500 --> 00:50:39.900
the answer will usually not be a good one.

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:42.300
But if you can present an error,

00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:45.390
if you can show what happens
when due process is deprived,

00:50:45.390 --> 00:50:48.930
when you do punishment
before trial, (chuckles)

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:52.980
then you can make people ask
a different question, right?

00:50:52.980 --> 00:50:57.210
Are we willing to have a
system like this operating

00:50:57.210 --> 00:50:59.790
in our name when we know
that there might be dozens

00:50:59.790 --> 00:51:03.750
or hundreds of people who
were swept up inadvertently

00:51:03.750 --> 00:51:05.950
because of the deprivation
of legal process?

00:51:06.810 --> 00:51:08.820
I think there was, there was some success.

00:51:08.820 --> 00:51:09.900
I don't want to be Pollyannaish here.

00:51:09.900 --> 00:51:12.453
Obviously, my talk was the
opposite of that, right?

00:51:13.650 --> 00:51:15.483
We completely failed in court.

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:18.940
There are some who have written books,

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:22.410
David Cole at Georgetown,
Jack Goldsmith at Harvard,

00:51:22.410 --> 00:51:24.780
who argue that all of
this failed litigation,

00:51:24.780 --> 00:51:27.120
in fact, succeeded in a way

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:28.980
in turning the public
against these activities,

00:51:28.980 --> 00:51:31.500
eventually getting Congress activated,

00:51:31.500 --> 00:51:34.860
making it politically impossible
for the CIA, in the future,

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:38.700
ever to agree to do
something like this again.

00:51:38.700 --> 00:51:40.860
That may or may not be
right. We don't know.

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:43.560
It's two ways of looking at
the same body of evidence.

00:51:43.560 --> 00:51:45.150
That was certainly the intent.

00:51:45.150 --> 00:51:49.800
The intent was to use litigation
as a public demonstration

00:51:49.800 --> 00:51:52.563
of what happens when you
abandon the rule of law.

00:51:56.672 --> 00:51:58.350
- [Audience Member 2] So, I'm
sure you're gonna answer this

00:51:58.350 --> 00:51:59.818
in tomorrow's lecture like you said.

00:51:59.818 --> 00:52:00.651
(Ben chuckles)

00:52:00.651 --> 00:52:02.460
But in our discussion of Poland,

00:52:02.460 --> 00:52:04.380
the thing I was thinking of was,

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:09.093
in 2023, what does constitute
a legitimate state secret?

00:52:10.530 --> 00:52:11.910
- In other words, what do I consider

00:52:11.910 --> 00:52:13.353
to be a legitimate secret?

00:52:14.520 --> 00:52:15.870
I mean, are you asking if I think

00:52:15.870 --> 00:52:18.028
that there are legitimate state secrets?

00:52:18.028 --> 00:52:19.740
- [Audience Member 2] Yeah.
- Okay. So I do, actually.

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:22.680
I think there are some who
would take the position

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:25.020
that there is no such thing

00:52:25.020 --> 00:52:26.280
as a legitimate government secret.

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:28.833
I think the category isn't large.

00:52:30.540 --> 00:52:32.760
Daniel Ellsberg recently gave an interview

00:52:32.760 --> 00:52:35.100
to the New York Times, in
which he said that he thinks

00:52:35.100 --> 00:52:37.590
maybe half of 1% of what's classified

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:40.020
should still be classified.

00:52:40.020 --> 00:52:42.810
But examples of what
is properly classified,

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:45.480
certainly the identities of our agents

00:52:45.480 --> 00:52:46.920
or informants overseas,

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:49.470
the disclosure of which
would lead to their deaths.

00:52:51.450 --> 00:52:54.150
We shouldn't be publishing
troop movements in advance.

00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:55.500
If a newspaper found out

00:52:55.500 --> 00:52:57.930
about the bin Laden raid the night before,

00:52:57.930 --> 00:52:58.950
it wouldn't be responsible

00:52:58.950 --> 00:53:01.560
to publish the article before it happened.

00:53:01.560 --> 00:53:03.990
Large troop movements can't
happen in secret anymore.

00:53:03.990 --> 00:53:06.690
There's no, there's not
gonna be another Normandy.

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:08.103
But still that's legitimate.

00:53:09.077 --> 00:53:13.500
And there may, certainly,
there may still be some secrets

00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:15.210
related to nuclear proliferation.

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:16.770
Not many.

00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:18.960
I think the real obstacle
to proliferation now

00:53:18.960 --> 00:53:20.820
is material and not information.

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:21.990
Most people have the knowledge.

00:53:21.990 --> 00:53:24.003
But I think those would be things

00:53:24.003 --> 00:53:26.550
that are legitimate state secrets.

00:53:26.550 --> 00:53:28.635
And I want to make another point,

00:53:28.635 --> 00:53:30.330
and I will get into this more tomorrow.

00:53:30.330 --> 00:53:33.570
Something might be a legitimate secret,

00:53:33.570 --> 00:53:36.930
but we don't have to treat
that assertion as absolute.

00:53:36.930 --> 00:53:40.830
In U.S. law, once a court accepts
the government's assertion

00:53:40.830 --> 00:53:42.676
that something is secret,

00:53:42.676 --> 00:53:46.710
say the U.S. relationship with Poland,

00:53:46.710 --> 00:53:48.750
that's the end of the inquiry.

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:50.857
But what a court really
ought to do is say,

00:53:50.857 --> 00:53:52.500
"What's at stake in this litigation?

00:53:52.500 --> 00:53:54.210
What are the countervailing principles

00:53:54.210 --> 00:53:55.410
that are at stake here?"

00:53:56.261 --> 00:53:58.290
A torture victim's right
to have his case heard,

00:53:58.290 --> 00:54:00.060
the system's need to have a resolution

00:54:00.060 --> 00:54:03.480
of an important legal question,
and balance those interests.

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:05.670
Whereas we don't have any
proportionality analysis.

00:54:05.670 --> 00:54:07.650
We don't have any balancing
in the U.S. legal system.

00:54:07.650 --> 00:54:11.400
Once a secrecy claim is accepted
as legitimate, that's it.

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:14.070
It basically extinguishes the
case that we're talking about.

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:16.500
So that would be another way

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:18.639
that I would respond to that question.

00:54:18.639 --> 00:54:20.104
- Yeah, so...
- Yeah, go ahead.

00:54:20.104 --> 00:54:22.140
- [Audience Member 2] So,
I guess just to clarify,

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:25.200
you're saying that these are
not necessarily enumerated?

00:54:25.200 --> 00:54:26.610
Like there's no list

00:54:26.610 --> 00:54:30.293
of what constitutes a
legitimate state secret in 2023?

00:54:30.293 --> 00:54:32.730
- That's, that's exactly
right, and in fact,

00:54:32.730 --> 00:54:34.440
I'll say even more than that.

00:54:34.440 --> 00:54:35.730
So, if the government

00:54:35.730 --> 00:54:38.250
is invoking the state secrets privilege,

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:39.810
the category that the law uses

00:54:39.810 --> 00:54:42.180
is national defense information.

00:54:42.180 --> 00:54:44.550
The definition of that is so broad

00:54:44.550 --> 00:54:47.010
that it refers to almost anything

00:54:47.010 --> 00:54:49.890
relating to the foreign
policy of the United States.

00:54:49.890 --> 00:54:51.570
If it's given its plain meaning,

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:53.523
it's clearly an unconstitutional law.

00:54:54.960 --> 00:54:58.560
Courts generally equate it
with classified information.

00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:01.350
But no, there's not anywhere you can find

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:03.090
an enumerated list of categories.

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:05.820
I mean, there are some
statutes that deal specifically

00:55:05.820 --> 00:55:07.560
with say, signals intelligence,

00:55:07.560 --> 00:55:10.980
and they would tell you with
a little bit more specificity.

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:13.080
But in general, the
state secrets privilege

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:15.600
is a judicially created doctrine.

00:55:15.600 --> 00:55:17.340
There isn't even a statute
that establishes it.

00:55:17.340 --> 00:55:18.667
The courts just said,

00:55:18.667 --> 00:55:22.440
"We're going to apply this
common law evidentiary privilege

00:55:22.440 --> 00:55:24.030
to give the government the ability

00:55:24.030 --> 00:55:25.377
to shut down these cases."

00:55:28.514 --> 00:55:29.850
- [Audience Member 3] So thank you for,

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:32.400
first, obviously, thank you
for coming to give this lecture

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:36.060
and the lecture tomorrow, but I think,

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:40.020
so given, so after listening
to this for around an hour now,

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:42.723
it's really depressing, (chuckles) right?

00:55:44.160 --> 00:55:45.120
And so I guess for you,

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:48.123
like after decades of doing this work,

00:55:49.080 --> 00:55:51.300
do you ever find yourself wondering

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:54.000
if there are other venues, other avenues,

00:55:54.000 --> 00:55:57.480
to be addressing these, in particular,

00:55:57.480 --> 00:56:00.390
these particular cases beyond
the court of public opinion?

00:56:00.390 --> 00:56:01.470
Beyond the judicial system?

00:56:01.470 --> 00:56:04.980
Especially, if we look at how
entrenched the judicial system

00:56:04.980 --> 00:56:06.900
and the political
legislative bodies have been

00:56:06.900 --> 00:56:09.843
over the past, a long time.

00:56:11.070 --> 00:56:14.610
- Yeah, I mean, so there are
courts in other jurisdictions.

00:56:14.610 --> 00:56:16.800
So we have helped present these cases

00:56:16.800 --> 00:56:20.250
to courts in other
countries. (clears throat)

00:56:20.250 --> 00:56:22.080
Paradoxically, there were,

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:26.280
a case that I didn't talk
tonight involved five citizens,

00:56:26.280 --> 00:56:27.600
some citizens of the United Kingdom,

00:56:27.600 --> 00:56:29.441
some citizens of Sweden,

00:56:29.441 --> 00:56:32.340
who were victims of the
CIA's rendition program.

00:56:32.340 --> 00:56:34.830
Their lawsuit was dismissed
in the United States

00:56:34.830 --> 00:56:36.360
on state secrecy grounds, on the ground

00:56:36.360 --> 00:56:38.700
that the CIA couldn't confirm
or deny its relationship

00:56:38.700 --> 00:56:41.100
with foreign partners.

00:56:41.100 --> 00:56:43.100
And the courts in those
other countries gave remedies

00:56:43.100 --> 00:56:44.160
to the same people.

00:56:44.160 --> 00:56:45.570
So they actually paid damages

00:56:45.570 --> 00:56:47.820
for their minor roles in these renditions.

00:56:47.820 --> 00:56:51.153
So, if you're talking
about a legal system,

00:56:52.140 --> 00:56:53.730
there are courts in other places.

00:56:53.730 --> 00:56:57.090
I also think part of this
is bearing witness, right?

00:56:57.090 --> 00:56:59.880
General Pinochet in Chile did not expect

00:56:59.880 --> 00:57:01.290
that he was gonna be arrested in London

00:57:01.290 --> 00:57:06.210
and spend his twilight years
fighting against accusations

00:57:06.210 --> 00:57:07.830
of what he had done decades earlier.

00:57:07.830 --> 00:57:10.680
So, you know, I mentioned that Bush

00:57:10.680 --> 00:57:13.320
and Cheney don't go to Europe right now.

00:57:13.320 --> 00:57:17.970
So part of this is shrinking
the world for those people.

00:57:17.970 --> 00:57:20.550
It's one form of accountability.

00:57:20.550 --> 00:57:25.140
I do think public opinion is vital,

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:27.270
because it creates the climate

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:29.580
in which the next president will decide

00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:33.633
whether or not to try something like this.

00:57:35.340 --> 00:57:40.340
But I, you know, I was deliberate.

00:57:40.710 --> 00:57:42.750
This was a record of failure, right?

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:47.220
Not my failure, the failure of
the system to give an answer

00:57:47.220 --> 00:57:49.530
to some of these most
fundamental questions.

00:57:49.530 --> 00:57:52.380
And the question I'm gonna
ask more pointedly tomorrow

00:57:52.380 --> 00:57:55.390
is, "Why are we putting this
body of law on a pedestal

00:57:56.520 --> 00:57:59.157
when it treats these vital
questions in these ways?"

00:58:00.393 --> 00:58:01.343
- [Audience Member 3] Thank you.

00:58:06.204 --> 00:58:07.928
- [Audience Member 4] So the fact

00:58:07.928 --> 00:58:09.780
that the court isn't
answering these questions,

00:58:09.780 --> 00:58:12.000
how much of that do you think is the fact

00:58:12.000 --> 00:58:15.120
that they're sort of
delaying the requirement

00:58:15.120 --> 00:58:17.310
that they come up with
a satisfactory answer

00:58:17.310 --> 00:58:18.870
'cause they they can't think of one,

00:58:18.870 --> 00:58:21.030
or because they already have an answer,

00:58:21.030 --> 00:58:23.380
and they just don't want
to have to justify it?

00:58:24.510 --> 00:58:25.533
- I, well.

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:31.530
The way that I've heard
judges talk about this,

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:34.770
and again, I'll get into this
tomorrow in some more detail,

00:58:34.770 --> 00:58:35.673
I actually will,

00:58:37.350 --> 00:58:40.050
is that when a senior leader

00:58:40.050 --> 00:58:43.717
of the intelligence
community says to a judge,

00:58:43.717 --> 00:58:46.710
"In my expert determination,

00:58:46.710 --> 00:58:48.900
if you take these steps,

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:51.510
it's going to have X, Y, and Z effect

00:58:51.510 --> 00:58:54.090
on our national security,"
two things are happening.

00:58:54.090 --> 00:58:58.170
First of all, the judge
does doubt his own judgment,

00:58:58.170 --> 00:59:01.050
doesn't necessarily want to be
the one to make that decision

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:02.400
in the face of that claim.

00:59:02.400 --> 00:59:04.220
The second, what's been
communicated to that judge

00:59:04.220 --> 00:59:06.840
is that if anything bad
happens, it's gonna be on you,

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:08.460
and all fingers are
gonna be pointed at you.

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:11.190
So it's a political comment as well.

00:59:11.190 --> 00:59:15.090
And the theatricality of some of the ways

00:59:15.090 --> 00:59:16.710
in which these secrecy claims are made

00:59:16.710 --> 00:59:19.590
with an executive branch security officer

00:59:19.590 --> 00:59:20.850
coming into the judge's chambers

00:59:20.850 --> 00:59:22.890
with a briefcase handcuffed to his wrist

00:59:22.890 --> 00:59:26.700
to deliver the classified
affidavit to the CIA Director.

00:59:26.700 --> 00:59:28.500
There aren't a lot of circumstances

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:31.777
in which judges are
going to say, you know,

00:59:31.777 --> 00:59:33.780
"That's nonsense."

00:59:33.780 --> 00:59:34.683
There are some.

00:59:37.230 --> 00:59:39.090
Where we just passed the 50th anniversary

00:59:39.090 --> 00:59:44.090
of the Pentagon Papers case,
where the Nixon administration

00:59:44.310 --> 00:59:47.730
almost persuaded a federal
judge to enjoin publication

00:59:47.730 --> 00:59:49.680
on the basis of the government's assertion

00:59:49.680 --> 00:59:52.740
that there would be grave
damage to national security.

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:55.260
The judge said later he
was expecting to enjoin it.

00:59:55.260 --> 00:59:57.960
He kept saying to the
government, "Where's the evidence

00:59:57.960 --> 00:59:59.700
that something bad is going to happen?"

00:59:59.700 --> 01:00:01.973
And the government wasn't
able to produce anything.

01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:05.250
More than a decade later,

01:00:05.250 --> 01:00:08.647
the government lawyer who
was arguing this case said,

01:00:08.647 --> 01:00:10.020
"Hey, I stood before the Supreme Court

01:00:10.020 --> 01:00:11.760
and said the publication
of these documents

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:13.140
were going to lead to grave damage.

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:15.810
I've never seen a scintilla of evidence

01:00:15.810 --> 01:00:17.640
of damage from their publication.

01:00:17.640 --> 01:00:19.170
I've never even seen anybody claim

01:00:19.170 --> 01:00:20.730
that there was damage
from their publication."

01:00:20.730 --> 01:00:23.040
So again, my argument
is that the judges need

01:00:23.040 --> 01:00:26.280
to be considering the
executive branch claims

01:00:26.280 --> 01:00:29.520
in the context of that
history of deception.

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:30.630
But it's not easy.

01:00:30.630 --> 01:00:32.670
The much easier thing
to do if you're a judge

01:00:32.670 --> 01:00:35.790
is to kick it up to the next level, right?

01:00:35.790 --> 01:00:37.560
There may be something
else going on as well,

01:00:37.560 --> 01:00:39.030
if you're the Supreme Court here,

01:00:39.030 --> 01:00:43.320
which is that it's a little odd

01:00:43.320 --> 01:00:45.813
to have an agency like
the CIA in a democracy.

01:00:47.370 --> 01:00:51.120
That is, we send agents
out into the world,

01:00:51.120 --> 01:00:52.593
basically, to break the law.

01:00:54.060 --> 01:00:57.810
And they don't wanna then be
dragged into a U.S. courtroom

01:00:57.810 --> 01:00:59.670
to have what they did adjudicated

01:00:59.670 --> 01:01:01.260
after they were sent
out to do these things.

01:01:01.260 --> 01:01:03.990
And so we come up with
ways of describing that,

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:06.580
like secrecy, but it's not secrecy.

01:01:06.580 --> 01:01:09.300
It's really just, you know, our trying

01:01:09.300 --> 01:01:11.610
to put some scaffolding on
a decision that we've made

01:01:11.610 --> 01:01:14.310
that these people are going
to operate outside the law,

01:01:14.310 --> 01:01:16.710
and it wouldn't be fair
to subject them to it.

01:01:16.710 --> 01:01:20.130
And again, I don't
think that's irrational.

01:01:20.130 --> 01:01:21.990
I just think it's terrifying, right?

01:01:21.990 --> 01:01:23.840
Because it has no limiting principle.

01:01:36.397 --> 01:01:38.400
- [Audience Member 5] So for a class,

01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:42.870
I was reading about Haitian refugees

01:01:42.870 --> 01:01:47.870
who were kept in the
Guantanamo prison camp,

01:01:47.910 --> 01:01:50.940
and I know there were some
recent sort of unconfirmed,

01:01:50.940 --> 01:01:52.230
I think last year reports

01:01:52.230 --> 01:01:55.200
that the Biden
administration was looking at

01:01:55.200 --> 01:02:00.200
keeping migrants in
Guantanamo, in that area.

01:02:01.650 --> 01:02:03.540
And I was just wondering

01:02:03.540 --> 01:02:07.170
whether there are sort of legal avenues

01:02:07.170 --> 01:02:12.170
to preventing sort of the
detaining of these types of,

01:02:15.690 --> 01:02:18.900
whether it's refugees
or political prisoners.

01:02:18.900 --> 01:02:21.480
Has the legal system sort
of evolved in any way

01:02:21.480 --> 01:02:23.475
to prevent the next Guantanamo?

01:02:23.475 --> 01:02:24.630
I'm not an expert on the
refugee side of things,

01:02:24.630 --> 01:02:27.840
but you should know that,
actually, law students and faculty

01:02:27.840 --> 01:02:30.870
here at Yale in the 1990s
brought successful challenges

01:02:30.870 --> 01:02:33.840
on behalf of the Haitian refugees
who were held there then,

01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:35.850
against the Clinton administration

01:02:35.850 --> 01:02:38.670
and did create legal
pathways for those people

01:02:38.670 --> 01:02:39.840
to be released from Guantanamo

01:02:39.840 --> 01:02:41.220
and admitted into the United States.

01:02:41.220 --> 01:02:46.220
And so that's quite a distinct
sort of category of law.

01:02:49.380 --> 01:02:52.410
As far as the remaining, you know,

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:55.680
sort of so-called War on
Terror detainees at Guantanamo,

01:02:55.680 --> 01:02:58.110
I think the best thing
that can be said about it

01:02:58.110 --> 01:03:01.740
is that it's been a very, very long time

01:03:01.740 --> 01:03:04.410
since anyone has been
brought to Guantanamo.

01:03:04.410 --> 01:03:07.590
So I think the political class

01:03:07.590 --> 01:03:09.120
and legal class have more or less decided

01:03:09.120 --> 01:03:11.910
they're just going to
let this problem age out.

01:03:11.910 --> 01:03:15.000
They're just going to let
the people who are there,

01:03:15.000 --> 01:03:17.370
you know, at an incredible cost,

01:03:17.370 --> 01:03:19.980
both in dollars and moral authority,

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:22.560
just grow old and die there,
the few that are left.

01:03:22.560 --> 01:03:25.200
And, you know, part of
what's made it so difficult

01:03:25.200 --> 01:03:27.210
to find homes, even for the ones

01:03:27.210 --> 01:03:29.490
who the U.S. says should be released,

01:03:29.490 --> 01:03:32.580
is that Congress passed a law saying

01:03:32.580 --> 01:03:35.130
that no one who's been in
Guantanamo can ever be admitted

01:03:35.130 --> 01:03:36.600
into the United States.

01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:39.270
So we accepted zero.

01:03:39.270 --> 01:03:41.647
And now we're telling all the
other countries in the world,

01:03:41.647 --> 01:03:42.720
"If you don't like Guantanamo,

01:03:42.720 --> 01:03:44.490
you've gotta take these people."

01:03:44.490 --> 01:03:48.510
But we've taken none of them, so you know,

01:03:48.510 --> 01:03:51.660
that has, obviously, made
it incredibly complicated

01:03:51.660 --> 01:03:55.290
to get 800 people who
were held there released.

01:03:55.290 --> 01:03:59.490
And then, and then, you know,

01:03:59.490 --> 01:04:01.950
the last thing I will
say is that, of course,

01:04:01.950 --> 01:04:05.730
we still are holding
the alleged perpetrators

01:04:05.730 --> 01:04:09.540
of 9/11 at Guantanamo and
their trial hasn't started,

01:04:09.540 --> 01:04:12.063
22 years after the attack.

01:04:13.741 --> 01:04:15.240
- [Audience Member 6] Why
hasn't the trial started?

01:04:15.240 --> 01:04:16.872
- Come tomorrow.

01:04:16.872 --> 01:04:20.160
(audience laughing)

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:21.684
I'm not kidding.
(audience laughing)

01:04:21.684 --> 01:04:23.070
- [Audience Member 6] Okay, I'll be here.

01:04:23.070 --> 01:04:24.963
- Okay, yeah.

01:04:28.020 --> 01:04:28.950
- [Staff Member] Can I have the mic?

01:04:28.950 --> 01:04:30.372
- [Ana] Mm-hmm.

01:04:30.372 --> 01:04:31.293
- [Staff Member] This side of the room.

01:04:42.879 --> 01:04:44.280
- [Audience Member 7] So Ben, just,

01:04:44.280 --> 01:04:46.503
I guess, a quick question.

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:49.650
Given that you mentioned Pinochet,

01:04:49.650 --> 01:04:53.550
so that made me think about the processes

01:04:53.550 --> 01:04:56.430
of truth and reconciliation
in Latin America

01:04:56.430 --> 01:04:59.223
and the end of dictatorships
in the Southern con.

01:05:00.612 --> 01:05:05.197
And part of that process
in some ways was a product

01:05:06.810 --> 01:05:11.670
of also internal splits
within the military,

01:05:11.670 --> 01:05:14.940
and younger cohorts being
tired of being accused

01:05:14.940 --> 01:05:18.270
for the crimes of the elder cohorts.

01:05:18.270 --> 01:05:20.940
I wonder if you see something like that

01:05:20.940 --> 01:05:23.550
happening within the CIA?

01:05:23.550 --> 01:05:28.350
Or, do you still see that as
a monolithic close agency?

01:05:28.350 --> 01:05:29.880
- I don't know enough to answer that.

01:05:29.880 --> 01:05:31.620
I mean, there were plenty of people

01:05:31.620 --> 01:05:34.110
in the military who objected.

01:05:34.110 --> 01:05:36.210
I mean, that's why we
know about Abu Ghraib.

01:05:36.210 --> 01:05:38.040
That was a military whistleblower.

01:05:38.040 --> 01:05:41.010
There were many uniformed
officers who came forward

01:05:41.010 --> 01:05:44.610
and said, "We can't torture prisoners

01:05:44.610 --> 01:05:47.130
because our people become prisoners."

01:05:47.130 --> 01:05:51.000
So, and you had people who
were still in the military

01:05:51.000 --> 01:05:53.430
who said that, and then you
had hundreds of officers

01:05:53.430 --> 01:05:55.500
who had retired from the military who,

01:05:55.500 --> 01:05:58.320
in brief after brief to the Supreme Court,

01:05:58.320 --> 01:05:59.790
you know, made this argument.

01:05:59.790 --> 01:06:03.960
So, there definitely were dissenters.

01:06:03.960 --> 01:06:07.500
The only evidence we have from
the CIA side is, you know,

01:06:07.500 --> 01:06:10.803
I mentioned there was a 2004
CIA Inspector General report.

01:06:12.330 --> 01:06:14.190
The reason why there's an
Inspector General report

01:06:14.190 --> 01:06:15.930
is that there were people, CIA people,

01:06:15.930 --> 01:06:19.980
who are on site saying,
"This is crazy illegal,"

01:06:19.980 --> 01:06:21.780
and reporting it to
their Inspector General.

01:06:21.780 --> 01:06:26.780
So, but all, you know,
except for brief moments,

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:30.120
all of those internal
investigations stay under wraps.

01:06:30.120 --> 01:06:33.390
They, you know, that 2004
report wasn't declassified

01:06:33.390 --> 01:06:36.000
until 2010 and still redacted.

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:38.040
And you see how the CIA fought

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:40.380
against the release of the
Senate's investigation,

01:06:40.380 --> 01:06:42.990
even hacking Senate investigators
to try to figure out

01:06:42.990 --> 01:06:45.330
what they knew about the
CIA's torture program.

01:06:45.330 --> 01:06:48.960
So, I don't know.

01:06:48.960 --> 01:06:52.533
I think, again, I think what's.

01:06:54.600 --> 01:06:58.410
I mean, anyone who has been
in a leadership position

01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:02.370
at the CIA knows how legally
tenuous their position is

01:07:02.370 --> 01:07:04.173
the minute that you let law inside.

01:07:05.040 --> 01:07:06.960
As soon as you let external law inside,

01:07:06.960 --> 01:07:10.293
so much of what they
do becomes problematic.

01:07:11.310 --> 01:07:13.920
You know, the former CIA General Counsel,

01:07:13.920 --> 01:07:15.987
his memoir was called "Company Man."

01:07:16.860 --> 01:07:19.470
Right, and I think that
better describes the attitude

01:07:19.470 --> 01:07:20.303
of people there.

01:07:26.390 --> 01:07:28.973
- [Ana] Back to the other side.

01:07:32.415 --> 01:07:33.248
- [Audience Member 8] Thank you.

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:38.130
So at a panel I watched

01:07:38.130 --> 01:07:40.590
shortly after the Snowden revelations

01:07:40.590 --> 01:07:42.420
with Chris Seghoian, actually,

01:07:42.420 --> 01:07:44.700
and oh, can you hear me okay?
- I hear you fine.

01:07:44.700 --> 01:07:47.200
- [Audience Member 8] Okay.
Yeah, and Mike Hayden.

01:07:48.140 --> 01:07:49.193
(Ben chuckles)
Mike Hayden said,

01:07:50.670 --> 01:07:52.980
he sort of acknowledged there
was a failure of transparency

01:07:52.980 --> 01:07:54.750
and that there was a need for,

01:07:54.750 --> 01:07:56.949
what he called "translucence", (chuckles)

01:07:56.949 --> 01:07:58.357
(Ben chuckles)

01:07:58.357 --> 01:08:01.500
which I take to be some
measured form of transparency.

01:08:01.500 --> 01:08:04.500
And I later read Edgar's book
(Ben chuckles)

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:05.610
where he also sort of claimed

01:08:05.610 --> 01:08:07.581
that there had been improvements

01:08:07.581 --> 01:08:09.093
in transparency post Snowden.

01:08:09.093 --> 01:08:10.350
- Tim Edgar's book?

01:08:10.350 --> 01:08:11.918
- Exactly, yeah.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:08:11.918 --> 01:08:13.541
- "Beyond Snowden", yeah.
- Okay, yeah.

01:08:13.541 --> 01:08:15.000
So my question to you is,

01:08:15.000 --> 01:08:17.640
Do you feel the trajectory of, you know,

01:08:17.640 --> 01:08:20.520
that there's been any
improvement in transparency,

01:08:20.520 --> 01:08:22.200
and perhaps, you know,
(Ben sighs)

01:08:22.200 --> 01:08:24.420
maybe comparing different parts

01:08:24.420 --> 01:08:26.078
of the intelligence community,

01:08:26.078 --> 01:08:29.280
including, you know, NSA,
and then, versus CIA or FBI.

01:08:29.280 --> 01:08:30.540
- I think I would make a different point,

01:08:30.540 --> 01:08:34.620
which is that almost without exception,

01:08:34.620 --> 01:08:37.140
the important things that we learn

01:08:37.140 --> 01:08:40.980
about secret government
activities come from leaks

01:08:40.980 --> 01:08:42.280
and not through oversight.

01:08:43.320 --> 01:08:45.810
So almost everything
important that we know

01:08:45.810 --> 01:08:48.870
about government misconduct
in the last two decades

01:08:48.870 --> 01:08:50.310
was accomplished because someone

01:08:50.310 --> 01:08:53.130
on the inside shared
information with the press,

01:08:53.130 --> 01:08:54.360
and the press published it.

01:08:54.360 --> 01:08:57.960
Now, even when things get
declassified by the government,

01:08:57.960 --> 01:09:00.240
they get declassified
because of public pressure

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:02.070
following leaks.

01:09:02.070 --> 01:09:03.960
I really think breaking the law

01:09:03.960 --> 01:09:07.410
is the engine for transparency.

01:09:07.410 --> 01:09:09.300
You know, you saw during
the Clinton administration,

01:09:09.300 --> 01:09:13.560
there was a kind of decent
push through a backlog

01:09:13.560 --> 01:09:16.200
of ancient, you know,
classified information.

01:09:16.200 --> 01:09:17.760
That's kind of not of interest to me.

01:09:17.760 --> 01:09:18.660
That's not what I'm talking about.

01:09:18.660 --> 01:09:21.030
I mean, interesting things come
out of that, and, you know,

01:09:21.030 --> 01:09:23.550
the National Security Archive
does a good job there.

01:09:23.550 --> 01:09:26.430
And there are, you know,
all kinds of people thinking

01:09:26.430 --> 01:09:28.200
about this problem of
the billions of pages

01:09:28.200 --> 01:09:29.760
of classified information from the past.

01:09:29.760 --> 01:09:34.380
But in terms of, you know,
shining a light on activities

01:09:34.380 --> 01:09:38.310
at a time that would give
the public an opportunity

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:40.203
to engage in democratic oversight,

01:09:41.460 --> 01:09:43.200
very rarely is it a revelation

01:09:43.200 --> 01:09:45.510
that comes out in, say, a public hearing

01:09:45.510 --> 01:09:47.760
of the Senate Intelligence
Committee, right?

01:09:47.760 --> 01:09:51.330
The really famous one
before Snowden was Clapper

01:09:51.330 --> 01:09:53.077
sitting before Ron
Wyden, and Wyden saying,

01:09:53.077 --> 01:09:55.350
"Is there any kind of
information that the NSA collects

01:09:55.350 --> 01:09:57.390
on millions, or hundreds
of millions of Americans?"

01:09:57.390 --> 01:10:00.537
And Clapper saying, you know,
"No sir, not wittingly."

01:10:01.740 --> 01:10:02.790
Right, and, you know, we all say

01:10:02.790 --> 01:10:04.350
that was Clapper lying to Congress,

01:10:04.350 --> 01:10:08.250
but really it was Congress
not doing oversight.

01:10:08.250 --> 01:10:10.290
Congress knew that it was a lie.

01:10:10.290 --> 01:10:13.110
We were the only ones who
didn't know it was a lie, right?

01:10:13.110 --> 01:10:15.510
So it's episodes like that,

01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:17.400
that convinced someone like Snowden

01:10:17.400 --> 01:10:19.800
there's actually not another way, right?

01:10:19.800 --> 01:10:21.510
There isn't a way through the system.

01:10:21.510 --> 01:10:23.190
Through the system is what
I just described to you,

01:10:23.190 --> 01:10:25.443
tediously tonight for an hour, right?

01:10:33.035 --> 01:10:36.090
- [Audience Member 9] Thanks. Thank you.

01:10:36.090 --> 01:10:37.950
I'm interested in the role

01:10:37.950 --> 01:10:41.310
that international criminal
justice might have to play here,

01:10:41.310 --> 01:10:45.480
and the current position of the U.S. that,

01:10:45.480 --> 01:10:50.340
well, the objection to
the ICC at all, basically,

01:10:50.340 --> 01:10:53.313
which is now having
ramifications because, you know,

01:10:54.210 --> 01:10:56.340
the U.S. won't share intelligence
and what Putin's doing,

01:10:56.340 --> 01:10:57.390
et cetera, et cetera.

01:10:58.680 --> 01:11:02.550
To what extent do you think
kind of a policy change there

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:07.550
would spill over and solve
any of these problems?

01:11:08.970 --> 01:11:12.960
- Well, it might, but what possible basis

01:11:12.960 --> 01:11:14.640
for optimism do we have here, right?

01:11:14.640 --> 01:11:17.430
So, I completely understand

01:11:17.430 --> 01:11:21.077
if you're in the U.S.
military right now saying,

01:11:23.197 --> 01:11:25.680
"We can't share information with the ICC

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:27.330
because the ICC open investigation

01:11:27.330 --> 01:11:29.220
of the U.S. in Afghanistan, right?

01:11:29.220 --> 01:11:30.660
And what did the U.S. do in response?

01:11:30.660 --> 01:11:33.937
We slapped sanctions on the
ICC prosecutors and said,

01:11:33.937 --> 01:11:35.880
"You can't come to the United States

01:11:35.880 --> 01:11:37.740
unless you drop your investigation

01:11:37.740 --> 01:11:39.090
of the U.S. in Afghanistan."

01:11:39.090 --> 01:11:44.090
So we can't say that these tribunals are,

01:11:44.160 --> 01:11:47.340
investigatory bodies are
illegitimate as applied to us,

01:11:47.340 --> 01:11:51.185
and then, you know, but what
a great place for Putin.

01:11:51.185 --> 01:11:53.700
(chuckles) I mean, I do
think that's what they think.

01:11:53.700 --> 01:11:55.819
But it actually is very difficult.

01:11:55.819 --> 01:11:57.810
And look, I mean, my thesis here

01:11:57.810 --> 01:12:02.460
is that impunity is the
organizing principle.

01:12:02.460 --> 01:12:04.530
And so, you know, even the need

01:12:04.530 --> 01:12:06.600
to provide a little bit more evidence

01:12:06.600 --> 01:12:08.940
against, you know, someone like Putin

01:12:08.940 --> 01:12:11.910
is not gonna be nearly strong
enough incentive for the U.S.

01:12:11.910 --> 01:12:15.390
to subject its own leaders

01:12:15.390 --> 01:12:18.603
to these kinds of tribunals, right.

01:12:22.680 --> 01:12:23.513
In the back.

01:12:34.849 --> 01:12:36.649
- [Audience Member 10]
It's a very brief question.

01:12:36.649 --> 01:12:37.732
(Ben chuckles)

01:12:37.732 --> 01:12:39.865
and if you'll answer it
tomorrow, then great.

01:12:39.865 --> 01:12:41.730
But I wonder if you think
we would be better off

01:12:41.730 --> 01:12:43.860
without all these organizations.

01:12:43.860 --> 01:12:45.868
- Which organizations?

01:12:45.868 --> 01:12:48.868
Oh, the three letter agencies?
- The CIA, the NSA, the FBI.

01:12:50.064 --> 01:12:51.450
- I guess I'm not willing to say that.

01:12:51.450 --> 01:12:53.130
I mean, the CIA is
probably the easiest case

01:12:53.130 --> 01:12:54.273
to say probably.

01:12:56.740 --> 01:12:59.500
The NSA with a properly constrained role

01:13:01.620 --> 01:13:04.530
is probably a useful thing
for a government to have.

01:13:04.530 --> 01:13:06.960
The FBI, certainly with a
properly constrained role.

01:13:06.960 --> 01:13:08.220
The FBI should be doing more

01:13:08.220 --> 01:13:11.190
to investigate financial
crime, for example.

01:13:11.190 --> 01:13:14.610
I think having all of these agencies focus

01:13:14.610 --> 01:13:16.563
the way they have on counter-terrorism,

01:13:17.610 --> 01:13:19.740
and indiscriminately counterterrorism,

01:13:19.740 --> 01:13:23.970
so from the sort of biggest
and most threatening iterations

01:13:23.970 --> 01:13:25.620
of that to the smallest, and most trivial,

01:13:25.620 --> 01:13:29.626
and even laughable, is
a huge, huge problem

01:13:29.626 --> 01:13:32.220
and risks a repetition of what you saw

01:13:32.220 --> 01:13:36.930
before the Church Committee
where the threat was communism.

01:13:36.930 --> 01:13:39.660
The backdrop of that was
nuclear annihilation,

01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:41.670
but the day-to-day FBI, CIA,

01:13:41.670 --> 01:13:44.280
and NSA files were
left-wing college professors

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:45.720
and black student organizations.

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:49.080
And that's not happening
now in that same way.

01:13:49.080 --> 01:13:51.450
But I think having these organizations

01:13:51.450 --> 01:13:56.450
adopt a counterterrorism,
homeland security kind of mandate

01:13:56.730 --> 01:14:00.093
in this really broad and non-specific way,

01:14:02.490 --> 01:14:05.610
and like using everything as
threat fuel to justify budgets

01:14:05.610 --> 01:14:08.793
and expansions is a major problem.

01:14:13.751 --> 01:14:17.501
(audience chattering softly)

01:14:20.940 --> 01:14:22.770
- [Ana] Are there any other questions

01:14:22.770 --> 01:14:24.253
from this side of the room?

01:14:26.850 --> 01:14:29.477
All right, so if there
are no more questions,

01:14:29.477 --> 01:14:33.657
I would like to thank
everyone for attending today.

01:14:33.657 --> 01:14:34.980
And I want to remind you

01:14:34.980 --> 01:14:37.590
that we're gonna continue
this conversation tomorrow

01:14:37.590 --> 01:14:39.900
at 4:00 PM, same room.

01:14:39.900 --> 01:14:41.220
The title for tomorrow's talk

01:14:41.220 --> 01:14:44.460
is "The Case Against
National Security" so...

01:14:44.460 --> 01:14:46.170
- I should warn you that I wrote the title

01:14:46.170 --> 01:14:47.250
before I wrote the lecture,

01:14:47.250 --> 01:14:49.530
